window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"t": "Blackline", "u": "index.html", "s": "A complete extraction-shooter interface for Unreal Engine 5.8: twenty-one CommonUI screens that you fill with data assets and Blueprints.", "h": ["The whole game UI.Already built.", "What you get", "See it filled first", "Start with a guide", "How it plugs into your game", "Know the limits"], "x": "UI plugin for Unreal Engine 5.8 The whole game UI. Already built. Blackline is twenty-one screens on CommonUI, from the hub to the raid debrief, with the stash, raid inventory, traders, market, hideout crafting, health and raid HUD in between. You fill them with data assets, one asset per item, trader, task, station and raid map. Most of it needs no C++. Get started See every screen How it fits together 21 screens 48 widget components 16 kinds of data asset 58 request tags your game can answer What you get Stash and paper doll Looting in a raid Hideout crafting Trading Raid HUD Lobby and deploy queue See it filled first A fresh install has no game content in it. Screens that list items, traders or stations show a short empty state card until you add some. To see every screen working, get the free Blackline Demo. It comes in three downloads. BlacklineDemo-Win64.zip is a playable Windows build. Unzip it and run BlacklineDemo.exe . BlacklineDemo-Linux.zip is a playable Linux build. Unzip it and run BlacklineDemo.sh . BlacklineDemo-Project.zip is the Unreal project with all its Blueprints and data assets, ready to open with the plugin and copy from. Add the free FPS Weapon Bundle from Fab first, as Getting started explains. The demo is a small playable raid loop. You pick a map, deploy, loot a crate and a body, extract, then craft, trade, heal and kit up at home. It is built only from Blueprints and data assets. Start with a guide Start here Getting started Enable the plugin, put the hub on screen from Blueprint and add your first data asset. Guide Items Item definitions with their types, traits, footprints and prices. Guide Weapons Attachment slots, magazines and the HUD's ammo readout. Guide Containers Bags, rigs and crates as grids that open in their own windows. Guide Context menu The right-click rows, each one a data asset with its click and key. Guide Character looks The wardrobe, try-on and buying. System Theming Colour roles, type steps and fonts on one style asset. Reference Requests Every request a screen raises and who answers it. How it plugs into your game Screens read your content from data assets and your live game state from sources, such as a Blueprint component on the player. When the player presses something that changes the game, the screen raises a request your game can answer. The plugin answers the common ones itself, such as cash, crafting, healing and tasks, so a game can get far without writing any request code. Your game Data assets and Blueprints Items, traders, tasks and maps as data assets. Live values such as health from a Blueprint component. values in → ← requests out Blackline Screens Twenty-one screens that show your values and turn the player's clicks into requests. draws → ← clicks The player Mouse and keyboard Drags, clicks and keys on the screen. Know the limits It is single player. The Inventory component does not replicate. The raid HUD's ammo readout shows the fitted magazine's capacity. It does not count shots. The FAQ covers the rest."}, {"t": "Getting started", "u": "getting-started.html", "s": "Enable Blackline, put the hub on screen from Blueprint, add your first data asset and get the demo project.", "h": ["Requirements", "Install and enable", "Show the hub", "What you see", "Add your first data asset", "The demo project", "Next"], "x": "This page takes you from a fresh install to a working hub screen, then to your first piece of content. Everything here is done in the editor and in Blueprint. You do not need C++. Requirements Unreal Engine 5.8. The plugin builds for Windows and Linux. A Blueprint or C++ project. Blackline works in both. Three engine plugins, which Blackline enables for you: CommonUI, ModelViewViewModel and EnhancedInput. Blackline needs no other plugin from its author. Its player subsystems are not created on a dedicated server. Install and enable Install from Fab Add Blackline to your library on Fab, then install it to your 5.8 engine from the Epic Games Launcher. Enable it Open your project, go to Edit > Plugins , find Blackline in the UI category and tick it. The editor enables CommonUI, ModelViewViewModel and EnhancedInput with it. Restart when asked. Use the CommonUI viewport In Project Settings > Engine > General Settings , set Game Viewport Client Class to CommonGameViewportClient . CommonUI routes its input through that class, and the demo project is set up the same way. The plugin's content mounts under /NVSBlackline/ . The screens are Widget Blueprints in /NVSBlackline/Screens/ , such as W_MainMenu and W_StashScreen . Show the hub One Blueprint node puts a screen on the viewport. It builds the layer stack for the player the first time it runs, so there is nothing to set up first. Open your player controller Blueprint Any Blueprint that knows the player controller works, such as your HUD or game mode. Add Show Screen By Tag on Begin Play Drag from Event BeginPlay and add Show Screen By Tag from NVS View Screen Library . Plug Self into Player and pick NVS.View.Screen.MainMenu as the Screen Tag . Press Play The hub opens over your level. Each screen class names the layer it belongs on, so the node never asks for one. The screen registry DA_BlacklineScreenRegistry maps every NVS.View.Screen.* tag to the Widget Blueprint that opens for it. A few more nodes on NVS View Screen Library cover most of what a game does with screens: Show Screen(Player, Screen) Opens a screen by its class instead of its tag. Close Screen By Tag(Player, ScreenTag) and Close Top Screen(Player) Closes one screen, or the topmost one as Escape does. Is Screen Open(Player, ScreenTag) True while that screen is showing. Open Loot Screen(Player, LootOwner) Opens the raid inventory with a crate's or a body's items beside the player's. Refresh Source(Player, Source) Redraws every open screen that reads a source, after its values change. If you prefer C++, the same functions are static members of UNVSViewScreenLibrary in the NVSViewWidgets module, such as UNVSViewScreenLibrary::ShowScreenByTag(PlayerController, ScreenTag) . Add \"NVSView\" and \"NVSViewWidgets\" to your Build.cs to call them. What you see The hub, as it looks with the demo's content in place. The hub opens on the Menu layer, so the cursor is free and gameplay input stops. Press Escape and it closes, because Escape removes the topmost screen. Your project has no content yet, so screens that list content tell you so. Open the traders from the hub and you see an empty state card titled No traders yet . Under it, outside a Shipping build, a line in the accent colour names what to create, here Trader Definition data assets. See Empty states for the full list. When the last screen closes, the plugin does not restore game input for you. Keep a HUD screen such as W_RaidHUD on the Game layer, or set your own input mode when you close the last menu. Add your first data asset Content in Blackline is data. Each item, trader, task, station or raid map is its own data asset, and the plugin finds them by itself. Here is a trader. Create the asset In the Content Browser, right-click and choose Miscellaneous > Data Asset . Pick Trader Definition and name it, for example PDA_Quartermaster . Fill it in Open it and set Display Name , Trade and Blurb . To give the trader a shelf, add rows to Shelf , each naming an Item Definition and its stock. Press Play and open the traders The empty state is gone and your trader has a card. There is nothing to register and no list to edit. Every field has a tooltip. The other kinds of data asset are listed on Architecture , and each has its own guide, starting with Items . The demo project The quickest way to see what a finished setup looks like is the free Blackline Demo. It is a small playable raid loop built only from Blueprints and data assets, so you can open any part of it and copy it into your game. BlacklineDemo-Win64.zip is a playable Windows build. Unzip it and run BlacklineDemo.exe . BlacklineDemo-Linux.zip is a playable Linux build. Unzip it and run BlacklineDemo.sh . BlacklineDemo-Project.zip is the project itself. Install Blackline The project lists the plugin, so install it into your 5.8 engine first. Add the FPS Weapon Bundle The demo's guns use the free FPS Weapon Bundle from Fab. It is not in the zip, because its licence does not allow passing it on. Add it to the project from Fab so it lands at /Game/FPS_Weapon_Bundle . Open and play Open BlacklineDemo.uproject . It opens on /Game/Maps/L_BlacklineDemo . Press Play and the hub opens. The demo also carries the example characters, outfits and hair (CC0 packs by Quaternius) as Wardrobe Piece Definitions. They are not part of the plugin. Without wardrobe pieces of your own, the character screens draw a mannequin of basic shapes. The places worth opening first: Folder What it shows Items , Traders , Tasks , Hideout , Wardrobe , RaidMaps One data asset per item, trader, task, station, outfit piece and raid map Demo/Sources BPC_Vitals and BPC_RaidClock , Blueprint components that feed live values to the screens Demo/Raid The crate, the dead operator, a loose item and the exit zone Demo The character, controller and game mode, with the raid flow in the controller's graph Next Start here Architecture How layers, screens, data assets, sources and requests fit together. Reference Screens All twenty"}, {"t": "Architecture", "u": "architecture.html", "s": "How Blackline is put together: layers, screens, data assets, sources, requests, empty states and modules.", "h": ["The short version", "Layers", "Screens and the screen registry", "Content is data", "Sources: live game state", "How data reaches a widget", "Requests", "Empty states", "Modules", "What it does not do"], "x": "Read this page to decide whether Blackline fits your game. It covers the pieces the plugin is made of, where a screen gets what it shows, and how a screen asks your game to change something. The short version Layers stack screens by purpose: the HUD, menus over play, full menus, dialogs and toasts. Screens are CommonUI widgets. Each screen class names the layer it opens on, and a screen registry maps a tag to each one. Data assets hold your content, one asset per item, trader, task, station, raid map and so on. The plugin finds them by itself. Sources hand live game state to the screens, such as health or the raid clock. The plugin has a built-in source for every screen, and you can swap any of them for a Blueprint of your own. Requests are how a screen asks for a change, such as Buy or Heal all. The plugin answers many of them itself. Empty states tell the player, and you, when a screen has nothing to show yet. Layers Every local player has one layer stack, held by UNVSViewLayerSubsystem . Which layers exist, their draw order and the input each takes come from a layout asset. The plugin ships DA_BlacklineLayout with five layers. Tag Holds NVS.View.Layer.Game The raid HUD NVS.View.Layer.GameMenu Screens over live play, such as the raid inventory and the task log NVS.View.Layer.Menu Full screen menus, such as the hub, stash, lobby and settings NVS.View.Layer.Modal The debrief, item windows, the match found banner and the drop countdown NVS.View.Layer.Notification Toasts and the item context menu The highest occupied layer that decides input sets the input mode. You never build the stack yourself: Show Screen By Tag , Show Screen and Open Loot Screen build it the first time they run. See Layers and screens . Screens and the screen registry Every screen derives from UNVSViewScreen , a CommonUI activatable widget. The plugin ships 21 screens and six windows and overlays, each a W_ Widget Blueprint under /NVSBlackline/Screens/ over a C++ class in NVSViewWidgets . They are listed on Screens . The screen registry DA_BlacklineScreenRegistry maps each NVS.View.Screen.* tag to the Widget Blueprint that opens for it, with an optional layer override. Screens open each other through it. To restyle or extend a screen, make a Blueprint child of its W_ asset, then point the registry row at your child. Everything that opens that screen now opens yours. Content is data You create content in the Content Browser with right-click > Miscellaneous > Data Asset . The plugin registers every one of these types with the Asset Manager when it starts, under every content root, so a new asset shows up in the screens with nothing to configure. Data asset One asset is Item Definition One item: name, footprint in cells, weight, price, category, a type and any traits. See Items . Item Category Definition A tab on the stash filter and the trade and market rails, such as Weapons or Medical. Item Action Definition One row an item's context menu can offer, with the click or key that does it. See Context menu . Trader Definition A trader, with their shelf, prices, barter costs and what they buy. Task Definition A job a trader gives, with the items to hand in and the reward. Station Definition A hideout station, with its levels, upgrade costs and recipes. Raid Map Definition A raid map: its level, briefing, conditions, insertion points, exits, raid time and its point on the region map. Loadout Definition A named kit: what is worn, what is fitted to the guns and what is packed where. Skill Definition, Perk Definition, Perk Branch Definition The skills screen and the skill tree. Wardrobe Piece Definition A cosmetic piece for one wardrobe slot, with its price. See Character looks . Notification Kind Definition A kind of toast, with its colour and how long it stays. Screen Source Definition Swaps one built-in source for yours, either a filled-in data asset or a class. View Setup Definition The theme, layer table, screen registry, icon rendering, money format and load tiers. Engine Settings Setup Definition The sound classes, input actions and other assets the settings screen drives. The two setup definitions replace what older versions kept in Project Settings. See Project settings . Sources: live game state Data assets describe things that do not change while the game runs. Values that do change, such as health, wind or the raid clock, come from sources . A source is any object that answers one of the plugin's interfaces. Each interface can be implemented in a Blueprint class or component. Interface Supplies NVS View Record Source Named records and lists of records NVS View Meter Source Figures with a range, such as health, wind or a wallet NVS View Container Source A grid's size and the items in it NVS View Slot Set Source A set of worn slots NVS View Filter Source The options on a filter rail NVS View Map Marker Source Points on a map NVS View Market Source The player market NVS View Setting Value Source Setting values, read and written NVS View Look Source What items and characters look like, for the icon renderer NVS View Item Holder A whole inventory, as the Inventory component holds one Every screen binding names a built-in source class, such as UNVSViewVitals for the HUD's vitals. Most built-in sources read the data assets or the player's Inventory component. When a screen opens, it picks each binding's source in this order: An object given to that one screen with SetBindingSource . The binding's SourceObject , set in the Widget Blueprint. An object given to every screen with Set Screen Source , from NVS View Definition Library , naming the built-in source it replaces. A Screen Source Definition data asset that replaces that built-in source. The built-in source itself. The usual Blueprint pattern is a component on the player that implements an interface, calls Set Screen Source on Begin Play, and calls Refresh Source whenever its values change. The demo's BPC_Vitals and BPC_RaidClock work this way. See Interfaces . How dat"}, {"t": "Connecting your game", "u": "interfaces.html", "s": "Show your game's own health, raid clock or cash on Blackline's screens with a small Blueprint component. No C++ needed.", "h": ["The idea in one minute", "Look at the demo's example first", "Build your own vitals component", "The names the screens look for", "Things to know", "Values that do not change while the game runs", "What else you can replace", "What you see", "Next steps"], "x": "Blackline's screens work out of the box with example values. To show your game's real values, such as the player's health or the time left in a raid, you add one small Blueprint component that hands them over. This page builds one step by step, and you will not need any C++. The idea in one minute Each screen gets its values from a source . Blackline ships a source for every screen, which is why a fresh install shows example numbers or an empty state. To connect your game, you give Blackline your own source for the values you care about. Everything else keeps working as it did. The raid HUD opens and needs the player's health, food and water. Blackline checks whether your game gave it a source for those values. If it did not, it uses its own. Your Blueprint component fills in a short list, such as health 72 out of 100. When health changes, your component calls Refresh Source and every open screen updates. Most of your content never needs this. Items, traders, tasks, stations and raid maps are data assets the screens find by themselves. A source is only for values your game changes while it runs. Look at the demo's example first The Blackline Demo project has two working components you can open and copy. Both are in /Game/Demo/Sources . BPC_Vitals hands over health, food, water and carried weight. BPC_RaidClock hands over the time left in the raid and on the exit. Open BPC_Vitals next to this page. Each step below matches something you can see in it. Build your own vitals component Create a Blueprint component In the Content Browser, right-click and pick Blueprint Class , then Actor Component . Add it to your player controller, so it lives as long as the player does. Add the interface Open Class Settings . Under Implemented Interfaces , add NVS View Meter Source . A meter is one value with a maximum, like health out of 100. Fill in your values In the My Blueprint panel, under Interfaces , open Get Meters . Add one Make NVS View Meter node per value. Set its Meter Id from the table below, its Value and Max Value from your game, and its Value Text to the text the player should read. Put the nodes into a Make Array , connect it to Out Meters and return true. Tell Blackline to use it On Begin Play , call Set Screen Source . Set Replaces to NVSViewVitals , which is Blackline's own vitals source, and connect Self to Source . Update the screens Whenever a value changes, call Refresh Source with your player controller and Self . A looping timer is fine too. BPC_Vitals refreshes four times a second. For health, food and water, tick Low Is Adverse so the bar warns as it runs low. The demo warns at half and turns red at a quarter, with a Warn Fraction of 0.5 and a Critical Fraction of 0.25. The names the screens look for A screen finds each value by its Meter Id . Use these names, and set Replaces to the source in the first column: Replaces Meter ids Shown on NVSViewVitals Vitals.Health , Vitals.Wind , Vitals.Food , Vitals.Water , Vitals.Carry Raid HUD, hub, stash, raid inventory, loadouts NVSViewRaidClock Raid.Clock , Raid.Exfil Raid HUD NVSViewWallet Wallet.OnHand , Wallet.StashValue Stash, trade, market The screens show Value Text exactly as you write it, so format it the way you want the player to read it. BPC_Vitals writes carried weight as 21.2 / 36.0 KG . A Max Value of zero means the value has no limit, like cash, and no bar is drawn. Things to know Call Set Screen Source before the first screen opens. A component on the player controller begins play before the controller does, which is early enough. One component feeds every screen that shows the same values. The raid HUD, the hub and the stash all show your health once it is connected. To go back to Blackline's own values, call Set Screen Source again with nothing connected to Source . Values that do not change while the game runs For data that is the same every time, such as the player's starting injuries, you can skip the Blueprint graph and use a Screen Source Definition asset instead. Right-click in the Content Browser, pick Miscellaneous > Data Asset , then Screen Source Definition , and fill it in: Replaces Which of Blackline's sources this stands in for, such as NVSViewBodyZones for the health screen. Asset A data asset of that same kind with your rows filled in. Class Or a Blueprint class that answers instead. Used only when Asset is empty. Sort Order When two definitions replace the same source, the lowest number wins. The demo's PDA_PlayerBodySource in /Game/Demo/Sources works this way. It points at DA_PlayerBody , which lists the player's fifteen body zones with their hit points and injuries, and the health screen shows them with no graph at all. If your game also connects a live source for the same values with Set Screen Source , the live one wins. What else you can replace These are the sources games most often swap for live values. Each one is replaced the same way as vitals. Replaces Blueprint interface What it shows NVSViewVitals NVS View Meter Source Health, wind, food, water and carried weight. NVSViewRaidClock NVS View Meter Source Time left in the raid and on the exit. NVSViewWallet NVS View Meter Source Cash on hand and stash value. It already follows the session's cash. NVSViewCompassMarks NVS View Record Source The marks on the HUD compass. NVSViewCrewVitals NVS View Record Source Your crewmates' rows on the HUD. NVSViewParty , NVSViewPartyChat NVS View Record Source The lobby's crew and chat. NVSViewMarketBoard NVS View Market Source The player market. Blackline's own board runs offline. The stash, the raid inventory and the paper doll need no source of yours. Add the Inventory component to the player's pawn and they follow it on their own. See Containers . Every interface, every field and a C++ version of the vitals component are on Source interfaces . What you see Play and deploy into a raid. The raid HUD shows your health, food and water, and they move as your game changes them. In the demo they run down during t"}, {"t": "Items", "u": "items.html", "s": "Make one Item Definition data asset per item, pick its type and traits, and it shows up in every grid, menu and inspect window.", "h": ["Make your first item", "The asset is found for you", "The fields every item has", "Item types", "Example values from the demo", "Traits", "Categories", "Items made at run time", "Result"], "x": "Every item in Blackline is one data asset, an Item Definition . You create it in the Content Browser, fill its fields in the Details panel, and the stash, the traders, the loot screens and the inspect window all read it. No code is needed. Make your first item Create the asset In the Content Browser, right-click and choose Miscellaneous > Data Asset . Pick Item Definition as the class. Give it a name that is unique in your project, such as PDA_AR4 . The name becomes the item's id. Fill the basics Set Display Name , Description , Footprint (cells wide by cells high), Value and Weight in kilograms. Tag it Add a gameplay tag to Tags that says what it is, such as Item.Weapon.Rifle . You make these tags yourself in Project Settings > Gameplay Tags . Categories, container rules and menu rows all read them. Pick a type Open the Type dropdown and choose one, such as Weapon . That type's own fields appear underneath. Give it a look Under Look , set Static Mesh to the item's mesh. The tile and the inspect window render it. Or set Icon to a texture if you already have a picture. Save The item now exists for every screen. You do not register it anywhere. Item tiles. The name, the category word, the weight and the rendered picture all come from the item's definition. The asset is found for you The plugin registers the Item Definition type with the Asset Manager when it starts. It scans every content root in the project, including game feature plugins, and marks the assets to always be cooked, because nothing references an item directly. You do not edit DefaultGame.ini . Everything else refers to an item by its primary asset id , which is the type NVSViewItem and the asset name, such as NVSViewItem:PDA_AR4 . Fields that take an item, such as a starting item or a trader's stock, show a picker that lists only items. Nothing loads until a tile shows it. Run Asset Actions > Validate Assets on your items. The definition reports mistakes such as a missing type, a weapon with no equip slot, an attachment with no kind or a trait listed twice. The fields every item has Display Name, Description The name on the tile and the paragraph in the inspect window. Footprint Cells it covers, width by height, before rotation. A 4 by 1 rifle lies flat. Value The price in whole currency units. The inspect window shows it as VALUE, and traders and the market use it. Weight Kilograms. Tiles show it times the stack count, and it counts toward carried weight while the item is worn or carried. Tags What the item is. Category tabs, container accept rules and menu rows match against these. Rarity A gameplay tag stored on the item for your game to read. The shipped screens do not use it yet. Type The kind of item. One per item. See below. Traits Extra behaviour any type can carry, such as stacking. See below. Icon A texture used instead of a rendered picture. Leave it empty to render one from the look. Look How the item is drawn on tiles, in the inspect window and on the body: a Static Mesh , a Skeletal Mesh (used when Static Mesh is empty, such as for trousers that follow the body) or a Pickup Actor (such as a fully built rifle Blueprint), plus an optional Framing override. See Character looks . Item types Each type adds its own fields under Type , and picking a type shows only that type's fields. The wearable types, Weapon, Armour, Container and Gear, also have Worn On and Worn Offset , which place the item on the character while it is worn. See Equipment . The type also gives the item its word on the tile when no category names it, such as WEAPON. Weapon Equip Slot (such as NVS.View.Slot.Primary or NVS.View.Slot.Sidearm ), Worn On , Worn Offset , Fire Modes and Attachment Slots . Each attachment slot has a Slot tag, a Label , an Accepts list of kinds, and a Socket and Offset for where a fitted part sits. The raid HUD reads the first fire mode of the worn primary. Calibre is stored for your game. See Weapons . Attachment Kind , such as Attachment.Optic . A weapon slot whose Accepts holds that kind takes it. Ammo Kind is the weapon slot a magazine loads into, such as Attachment.Magazine.AR4 . Capacity is the rounds it holds, which the raid HUD shows while it is fitted. Leave both empty for loose rounds. Calibre is stored for your game. Armour Equip Slot , such as NVS.View.Slot.Armour or NVS.View.Slot.Head , Worn On and Worn Offset . Armour Class and Protected Zones are stored for your game. Consumable Heals is the hit points it gives back to a body zone on the health screen. Food and Water are added when the player uses it from a grid. Uses , Use Seconds and Effect are stored for your game. Consumables are the items that get a Use row. Container Grids (a label and a size each, so a rig can have several pouches), Equip Slot (such as NVS.View.Slot.Pack , empty for a crate), Worn On , Worn Offset , Accepts (a tag query, empty takes anything), Refusal Text and Place . See Containers . Gear Equip Slot , Worn On and Worn Offset for worn gear such as a headset, gloves or trousers. Leave Equip Slot empty for loose gear such as salvage or grenades. Key Opens and Uses , stored for your game. Quest Item Task , the task definition that wants it, stored for your game. The fields marked as stored for your game are kept on the asset and readable in Blueprint, but no shipped screen acts on them yet. Read them from the definition when your game needs them. Example values from the demo The free Blackline Demo project builds its items from a table. A few of its rows, as you would type them in the Details panel: PDA_AR4 Weapon 4 x 1 3600 3.4 kg Item.Weapon.Rifle Equip Slot NVS.View.Slot.Primary, Fire Modes Semi, Auto Attachment Slots: OPTIC accepts Attachment.Optic, MAG accepts Attachment.Magazine.AR4 PDA_AR4Mag Ammo 1 x 2 240 0.5 kg Item.Ammo Kind Attachment.Magazine.AR4, Capacity 30, Stackable 3 PDA_MedKit Consumable 2 x 2 1200 1.1 kg Item.Medical Heals 60, Uses 3 PDA_Ration Consumable 1 x 1 70 0.4 kg Item.Food Food 30, Stackable 4 PDA_ChestRig Co"}, {"t": "Weapons", "u": "weapons.html", "s": "Make guns, magazines and scopes as data assets, fit parts by dragging, and show the gun on the raid HUD and in the player's hands.", "h": ["Make the gun", "Make the parts", "Fit a magazine or a scope", "Fitted parts show on the gun", "The raid HUD weapon readout", "Show live ammunition", "The gun in the player's hands", "Optional looks for items that are not definitions", "Result"], "x": "A weapon in Blackline is an Item Definition whose type is Weapon. This guide makes a carbine, its magazine and a scope from the free FPS Weapon Bundle on Fab, fits the parts by dragging, and shows the result on the raid HUD and in the player's hands. You need no C++ for any of it. Make the gun Add the FPS Weapon Bundle from Fab to your project. Its meshes land under /Game/FPS_Weapon_Bundle/Weapons/Meshes/ . Any static mesh of your own works the same way. Create the asset In the Content Browser, right-click and pick Miscellaneous > Data Asset , then Item Definition . The demo names its carbine PDA_AR4 . Fill the item fields Set Display Name , Description , Footprint (4 by 1 for a rifle), Value , Weight and Tags . A tag such as Item.Weapon.Rifle puts it under your Weapons category. Pick the type Set Type to Weapon . The weapon's own fields appear under it. Give it a look Under Look , set Static Mesh to SM_AR4 . Tiles, the paper doll and the inspect window now render that mesh. To use a flat picture instead, set Icon . The Weapon type has these fields: Equip Slot Where it is carried: NVS.View.Slot.Primary , Secondary , Sidearm or Melee . This is what puts Equip in the item menu. The editor reports an error when it is empty. Calibre A tag of your own, shown on the inspect sheet as the calibre. Fire Modes Picked from a list: Semi , Burst , Auto , Bolt and Pump . The first is the one the gun starts in. The inspect sheet lists them all and the raid HUD shows the first, as SEMI or AUTO . Attachment Slots The places a part can go. Each slot has a Slot tag that must be unique on the gun, a Label such as MAG , an Accepts list of part kinds, and a Socket and Offset that say where a fitted part sits on the mesh. The demo's AR-4 has two slots. The optic rail accepts Attachment.Optic . The magazine well accepts Attachment.Magazine.AR4 , so only the AR-4's own magazine fits it. The bundle's meshes carry no sockets, so the demo places each slot with Offset alone. Moving those points until a part lines up is covered in Placing weapon parts . Make the parts Parts are Item Definitions too. Two types can be fitted to a gun. Attachment One field, Kind , such as Attachment.Optic . A slot takes the part when its Accepts list matches the kind. An attachment with no kind fits nothing, and the editor says so. Ammo Calibre , Kind and Capacity . A magazine sets Kind to the slot kind it loads into and Capacity to its rounds. Loose rounds leave both empty. The demo's parts, all from the bundle: Asset Type Kind Capacity Mesh PDA_AR4Mag Ammo Attachment.Magazine.AR4 30 AR4/SM_AR4_Mag PDA_KA47Mag Ammo Attachment.Magazine.KA47 30 Ka47/SM_KA47_Mag PDA_SMG11Mag Ammo Attachment.Magazine.SMG11 20 SMG11/SM_SMG11_Mag_X PDA_Scope Attachment Attachment.Optic Accessories/SM_Scope_25x56_X Add the Stackable trait to a magazine if you want several in one cell. The demo stacks them three high. Every tile is rendered from the item's look, at the size of its footprint. Fit a magazine or a scope The player fits a part by dragging it onto any of these: The gun's tile in a grid. The worn gun on the paper doll. A slot in the gun's inspect window. The part goes into a slot that accepts its kind and is still empty. Dragging one magazine off a stack fits one and leaves the rest where they were. Dragging the part out again takes it off. In Blueprint, the Inventory component's Attach Item does the same. It takes the part's instance, the gun's instance and the slot tag, and returns false when the slot does not take that kind or is already full. A kit can start with parts already fitted. In a Loadout Definition or in the component's Starting Items , set Fitted To Slot on the magazine to the slot the gun is worn in. The demo's starter kit fits an AR-4 magazine to the gun worn in NVS.View.Slot.Primary , so the player starts with a loaded carbine. Fitted parts show on the gun Once a part is fitted, the gun's picture shows it everywhere the gun is drawn: its tile, the doll, the inspect window, the character figure and the gun in the player's hands. Each part sits at its slot's Socket and Offset . Icons are cached by everything in the look, so fitting a scope renders a fresh icon by itself. The inspect window lists the gun's slots under it. Drop a part on a slot to fit it there. Worn guns on the doll. A part dropped on a worn gun is fitted to it. How the gun hangs on the character's body while worn comes from Worn On and Worn Offset on its type. That, and lining up each slot, is the subject of Placing weapon parts . The raid HUD weapon readout The raid HUD ( W_RaidHUD ) shows the gun in hand from its Weapon binding. The built-in source is UNVSViewHeldWeapon . With an Inventory component on the player's pawn it reads the gun worn in its Held Slot and shows: The gun's name. Its first fire mode, such as SEMI . The fitted magazine's capacity as rounds, such as 30/30 . How many more magazines of the same item the player carries, such as ×6 MAG . Magazines in the stash do not count. Held Slot defaults to NVS.View.Slot.Primary . With nothing worn there the readout shows Unarmed Text , which defaults to Unarmed. The raid HUD. The weapon readout reads the worn primary and its fitted magazine. Blackline does not count shots. The readout always shows the fitted magazine full. To show live ammunition, give the HUD your own source as described below. Show live ammunition Make a component Create a Blueprint Actor Component, for example on your player controller, and add the interface NVS View Record Source . Answer Describe Record Fill the record: Headline for the gun's name, Status Text for the fire mode, Secondary Value for rounds left, Secondary Suffix for the capacity such as /30 , and Primary Value for spare magazines such as ×2 MAG . Return true. Stand in for the built-in source On Begin Play, call Set Screen Source with Replaces set to NVS View Held Weapon and Source set to self. Redraw after a change After a shot, a reload or a fire mode switch, call Refresh "}, {"t": "Placing weapon parts", "u": "placing-weapon-parts.html", "s": "Line up magazines, scopes, a gun worn on the back and the gun in the player's hands, using sockets or offsets.", "h": ["What moves what", "Place a magazine or a scope", "With a socket (recommended)", "With an offset only", "Place the gun on the character", "Place the gun in the player's hands", "When a part will not line up", "Result"], "x": "A fitted magazine or scope, a rifle slung on the back and the gun in the player's hands are all placed by numbers on your data assets. This guide shows where each number lives, which view it moves, and the fastest way to line them up. What moves what Three fields place everything. Change one and every view that uses it follows. Attachment slot: Socket and Offset On the weapon's Item Definition, under Type > Attachment Slots . Places a fitted part, such as a magazine or a scope, on the gun. It moves the part on the gun's tile picture, in the inspect window, on the gun worn on the character and on the gun in the player's hands. Worn On and Worn Offset On the weapon's Item Definition, under Type . Places the gun on the character's body while it is equipped, such as across the back or on the thigh. Its fitted parts ride along with it. The held gun component On your player Blueprint. Places the gun in front of the camera. In the demo it is the HeldWeapon component on BP_DemoCharacter . Fix the attachment slots first. They show up in the most places, and the worn gun and the held gun both inherit them. Place a magazine or a scope You have two ways to say where a fitted part goes. A socket on the gun's mesh is the cleanest, because you place it by eye in the mesh editor. An offset alone works on any mesh without opening it. With a socket (recommended) Open the gun's mesh Find the mesh the Item Definition's Look > Static Mesh points at, and double-click it. In the demo the AR-4 is /Game/FPS_Weapon_Bundle/Weapons/Meshes/AR4/SM_AR4 . Add a socket Open Window > Socket Manager and click Create Socket . Name it after the slot, such as Mag or Optic . Preview the part on it Select the socket and set Preview Static Mesh in its details to the magazine or scope mesh. Move and rotate the socket with the gizmo until the preview sits where it should. Save the mesh. Point the slot at the socket Open the weapon's Item Definition. Under Type > Attachment Slots , expand the slot, set Socket to the socket's name and set Offset back to zero location, zero rotation and a scale of 1. Save. Check it Start a new Play session, open the stash and look at the gun's tile, its inspect window (double-click the gun) and the doll. The part sits on the socket everywhere. The Offset still applies on top of the socket, so you can keep the socket where it is and nudge one gun's part with a small offset. With an offset only Leave Socket as None and type the part's place into Offset . The numbers are in centimetres and degrees, measured from the gun mesh's pivot, along the gun mesh's own axes. Find the gun's axes Open the gun's mesh and look at the axis widget. In the FPS Weapon Bundle the barrel runs along +Y and up is +Z . Measure the spot Hover over the magazine well or the rail in the mesh editor. Note how far it is from the pivot along each axis. Type the offset In the slot's Offset , set Location to that spot. Set Rotation so the part's own mesh lines up with the gun. Rotation reads X roll, Y pitch, Z yaw. The demo's rifle slots use a yaw of 90 because the part meshes face along X while the guns face along Y. Check and repeat Start a new Play session and open the inspect window. Nudge a centimetre at a time until it seats. The demo's starting values, as a reference point: Gun Optic slot location Mag slot location Yaw AR-4 (0, 5, 9.5) (0, 10, -2) 90 KA-47 (0, 8, 7.5) (0, 12, -3) 90 SMG-11 (4, 0, 9) (2, 0, -4) 0 Each slot accepts parts by tag. A magazine fits the AR-4's well because the slot's Accepts holds Attachment.Magazine.AR4 and the magazine's Ammo type has that Kind . Moving a slot never changes what fits it. Place the gun on the character A weapon shows its own Look mesh on the body, so you only set where it hangs. Its fitted parts follow it there. Pick a bone Set Worn On to a bone or socket on the character's skeleton. The demo slings rifles on spine_03 , the knife on spine_03 as well and the SMG on thigh_r . Open the character's skeleton to see every bone name. Set the offset Worn Offset is measured from that bone, along the bone's axes. The demo's rifles use location (0, -22, -2) with rotation Y 90 and Z 35, which lays them diagonally across the back. Check it Equip the gun and look at the doll on the stash or the character screen. A wrong bone name puts the gun at the character's feet, and the log says which name it could not find. A scope or magazine on a worn gun keeps the place its slot gives it on the gun, socket included, so a slot you fixed once is right here too. Place the gun in the player's hands The held gun is a Static Mesh component on your player that Show Held Item fills with the equipped weapon and its parts. The demo calls it whenever the inventory changes. Open the player Blueprint In the demo that is /Game/Demo/BP_DemoCharacter . Select the HeldWeapon component. It sits under the Eyes camera, so it moves with the view. Set where it sits Change Location . X is forward from the camera, Y is right, Z is up. The demo uses (68, 19, -27) , low and to the right. Set where the barrel points The demo turns the gun every frame so its barrel points at what the crosshair is on, 20 m ahead. That overrides the component's own Rotation. To change the gun's angle, open the Event Graph, find the group labelled \"The hand holds the weapon, and the barrel points at the crosshair\" and edit the A value of the Compose Rotators node. The demo uses (0, -90, 0) because the bundle's barrels run along +Y. Add a few degrees of roll or pitch here to cant the gun. Check it Play, deploy into a raid and look down the gun. Fitted parts sit where their slots say, because Show Held Item reads the same slots as every other view. The demo's Arms component is hidden. Its only animation is a pistol idle, which cannot hold a rifle. To show hands, give Arms a rifle animation of your own, untick Hidden in Game , and move HeldWeapon until the grip meets the hand. When a part will not line up The part sits at the gun's pivot The slot names a "}, {"t": "Containers", "u": "containers.html", "s": "Put the Inventory component on a player, crate or body, and get grids, worn bags, bags in bags, searching, loot rules, saving and starting kits.", "h": ["Give the player an inventory", "Base grids and worn grids", "How the stash screen maps its grids", "Bags inside bags", "Container windows", "What a container takes", "Crates and bodies", "Searching", "Loot rules", "Moving between inventories", "Starting items", "Blueprint calls", "Save and load", "Result"], "x": "Blackline ships an Inventory component that holds items for any actor: the player, a supply crate or a dead body. Add it, give it starting items, and the stash, the raid inventory and the loot screens fill themselves from it. Give the player an inventory Add the component Open your player pawn Blueprint, click Add in the Components panel and choose Inventory (class UNVSViewInventoryComponent ). Check the base grids In the Details panel, Base Containers already holds a Stash of 10 by 40 cells and Pockets of 4 by 1. Change the sizes or add more. Give it items Add rows to Starting Items , or set Starting Loadout to a Loadout Definition. See Starting items below. Press Play and open the stash A screen that shows the player's inventory looks for an Inventory component on the owning pawn when it opens. The stash grid, the pockets, the doll and the worn bags all read from it. To show another actor's inventory on a screen, call Set Inventory Owner on the screen with that actor. It finds the actor's first component that holds items. A screen whose Shows Player Inventory is ticked does this for the pawn by itself. Base grids and worn grids The component has two kinds of grid. Base containers are always there, such as the stash and the pockets. Each row has an Id , a Label , a Size , a Place (such as NVS.View.Place.Stash or NVS.View.Place.Pockets ), an Accepts tag query and a Refusal Text . Container items bring their own grids. An item whose type is Container opens every grid its definition lists, whether it is worn or carried, so a backpack keeps its contents when it comes off. A worn container's items sit in the place its type names, or in the place named like its slot. A pack worn in NVS.View.Slot.Pack holds items in NVS.View.Place.Pack , a rig in the rig's place. The menu rows and quick moves use these places to know where an item is. How the stash screen maps its grids Each container binding on a screen shows the grid of the same name, unless the screen's Inventory Targets map says otherwise. The stash screen ships with this map: Stash, Pockets The base containers with those ids. Pack The first grid of whatever is worn in NVS.View.Slot.Pack . RigMag, RigAdmin, RigMed Grids 0, 1 and 2 of whatever is worn in NVS.View.Slot.Rig . A rig draws its first grid on top and its other pouches in a row under it. Secure The first grid of whatever is worn in NVS.View.Slot.Secure . With nothing worn in a slot, its block stays on screen and its grids fold away until something is worn there again. Bags inside bags A container item can sit inside another container, such as a looted rig carried in your pack or an ammo crate in the stash. Double-click it, or choose Open in its menu, and its grids open in a window. Items inside it move with it. A bag can never go inside itself or inside anything it contains, at any depth. The component refuses such a move. A container window. It can be moved and resized, and items drag between it and the screen beneath. Container windows Each worn block on the stash screen has a pop-out button that opens its grids in a movable window. The screen's Pop Outs list names each button and the bindings its window shows. On the stash screen the pockets and the secure case are marked fixed, so they cannot be taken off. Opening the same container again brings its window to the front, and the front window is where Ctrl-click and the menu's Move to row send an item. What a container takes A container's Accepts is a gameplay tag query matched against the item's Tags . Empty takes anything. When the player drops an item it refuses, a REFUSED toast shows the container's Refusal Text , or a line saying the container does not take that item. The demo's secure case takes only small valuables. Its query matches any of Item.Key , Item.Medical , Item.Loot and Item.Quest , and its refusal text reads \"The lined tin takes keys, medicine and valuables only.\" Crates and bodies Put the same Inventory component on a crate or a body actor. Then open the raid inventory with it beside the player's gear: Add the component Add Inventory to the crate or body Blueprint. Clear Base Containers if it should only hold what it wears, or give it one grid for a crate. Name it Set Display Name , such as SUPPLY CRATE or DEAD OPERATOR . The loot pane's heading shows it. Tick the options Tick Needs Search to veil its grids until searched. On a body, tick Applies Loot Rules . Open it When the player interacts with it, call Open Loot Screen on NVS View Screen Library with the player controller and the actor. With no actor it opens the player's gear and hides the loot pane. The raid inventory. The player's gear is on one side and the crate or body on the other, with tabs for each. On a body, what it wears counts as loot. Its worn gear shows on a second doll, and its menu offers a Take into row, which moves it into your gear, instead of Unequip. Searching With Needs Search ticked, each grid shows a veil. The player clicks it to start a search with a progress bar, and clicks again to cancel. Each container is searched once and stays open after that. The time comes from the Setup Definition: Search Base Seconds 0.8 seconds for every search. Search Seconds Per Cell 0.12 seconds added for each cell of the grid. Search Body Factor 0.6. A body's gear takes this share of the time a crate of the same size would. Is Searched tells you whether a container's contents show yet, and Mark Searched opens one from your own code. Loot rules With Applies Loot Rules ticked, the Loot Rule trait on each item decides what can leave. StaysOnBody The item cannot be taken, but what is inside it can. The demo puts this on gloves and trousers. Trying shows \"That stays on the body. What is inside it can be taken.\" Sealed Nothing about the item can be taken or opened, including anything inside it. The demo puts this on the secure case. Trying shows \"It is locked. Only its owner can open it.\" Worn items that cannot leave show as fixed on the body's doll. Movi"}, {"t": "Context menu", "u": "context-menu.html", "s": "Every row of the item menu is an Item Action Definition data asset with its own label, gesture, key and rules. Add, change or hide rows without code.", "h": ["How it opens", "The rows that ship", "What a row holds", "What the queries match", "Targets", "Add your own row", "Change or hide a shipped row", "Result"], "x": "Right-clicking an item opens a menu of what the player can do with it where it sits. Each row is an Item Action Definition data asset, so you add a row, change a shortcut or hide a row by making an asset. No code is needed. The item menu. Greyed rows are actions that cannot happen right now, such as an equip with the slot already taken. How it opens Right-click a tile in a grid, a filled doll slot or a worn container block. The menu opens at the pointer above every window, and it stays inside the screen edges. Any click outside closes it. Choosing a row closes it and does the action. Rows can also be chosen without the menu, by their gesture: Ctrl-click, Alt-click, Shift-click, double-click or a key over the item. That is how every shortcut on the Drag and drop page works. The rows that ship The plugin ships seventeen rows in Content/Settings/ItemActions , listed here in their sort order. A row shows only when the item matches its Offered When . Open (double-click) A bag or crate lying in a grid, or a worn container with a pop-out. Opens its grids in a window. Not on a trader's shelf or a teammate's gear. Use A consumable lying in a grid. Not on a trader's shelf or a teammate's gear. Detach An attachment fitted to a weapon. Moves it to the first place with room. Inspect (double-click) Every item. Opens the inspect window. An item that opens a container keeps double-click for Open, because the first row to claim a gesture keeps it. Equip · SLOT (Alt-click) An item in a grid that names a slot. Greyed out when the slot is taken. Move to WINDOW (Ctrl-click) An item in a grid while a container window is open in front. Moves it into that window. Move to your gear (Ctrl-click) An item in the stash. Moves it to the rig's pouches, the pockets or the pack, the first with room. Shown only when no earlier row took Ctrl-click. Move to stash (Ctrl-click) An item in the pack, rig or pockets. Shown only on a screen with a stash, and only when no earlier row took Ctrl-click. Unequip (E) A worn item that is not fixed. Greyed out when the stash has no room. Take into GEAR (Ctrl-click) Loot from a crate or body. Moves it into your gear. Move to rig, Move to pack Between the worn pack and rig. Buy · PRICE An item on a trader's shelf. Sell · OFFER An item in the stash. Rotate (R) The player's own loose items. Greyed out unless the item lies in a grid. Split stack (Shift-click) The player's own loose items. Greyed out unless there is more than one in the stack. Drop (Delete) The player's own items that are not worn, fixed or fitted. Edits to the plugin's own assets are replaced when you update the plugin. To change a shipped row, make your own asset that replaces it, as shown below. What a row holds Open any of the shipped rows to see these fields in the Details panel. Action The request the row raises, as a gameplay tag, such as NVS.View.Request.Item.Drop . Two tags are handled by the screen itself: NVS.View.Action.Inspect opens the inspect window and NVS.View.Action.Open opens a container. Any other tag, including one of your own, is raised as a request. Label The row's text. It may name {Target} , {Slot} , {Price} (the shelf price) and {Offer} (what a sale fetches). Short Label Shown instead when a name in the label has nothing to fill it, such as Move to your gear when there is no target name. Key Hint The text at the right of the row, such as CTRL CLICK . Gesture, Key The shortcut that picks the row: None, Double Click, Ctrl Click, Shift Click, Alt Click, or Key with a Key such as R. Only When Gesture Free Hides the row when an earlier row already took its gesture. Otherwise the row stays and loses its shortcut and hint. Role The row's colour from the theme, such as Danger for Drop or Success for Sell. Offered When A gameplay tag query. Empty offers the row for every item. Enabled When A second query. When it does not match, the row shows greyed out. Target, Target Place What the row acts on, which also decides when it has room. See below. Hidden Without Place Hides the row on a screen that has no grid in Target Place . Replaces A row this one stands in for. Leave Action empty to hide that row. Sort Order Lower comes first. The shipped rows use 10 to 170 in steps of 10. What the queries match Offered When and Enabled When are matched against the item's own Tags plus two kinds of tag the screen adds: NVS.View.Place Where the item is: Stash , Pack , Rig , Pockets , Secure , Loot , Trader , Worn , Crew (a teammate's gear, view only), Attached or Wardrobe . NVS.View.ItemState What it is like now: InGrid , Stack , Usable , Opens , Fixed , Equippable or Attachable . Get Item Tag Set on NVS View Screen Library returns this combined set for an item, which helps when testing a query. Targets None The item itself. Fits Slot The doll slot the item fits. Acts when that slot can take it. Worn Slot The slot the item is worn in. Acts when Target Place , if set, has room. Open What the item opens. Not offered when it opens nothing. Front Window The container window in front. Not offered without one, or when it shows the item's own container. Gear The first of the rig, the pockets and the pack with room. Not offered on a screen without a pack. Place Target Place . Acts when it has room. Detach The first place with room for a fitted attachment. Add your own row This example adds a Repair row for weapons in the stash. Make a tag In Project Settings > Gameplay Tags , add Game.Item.Repair . Create the asset Make a Data Asset of class Item Action Definition , such as DA_ItemAction_Repair , anywhere in your Content folder. The plugin finds it by itself. Fill it Set Action to Game.Item.Repair , Label to Repair and Sort Order to 145, so it sits between Sell and Rotate. Set Offered When to a query that needs all of Item.Weapon and NVS.View.Place.Stash . Give it a key if you like Set Gesture to Key, Key to a free key and Key Hint to its name. Answer it In your player controller, get the NVS View Request Subsystem with Get Local Pla"}, {"t": "Drag and drop", "u": "drag-and-drop.html", "s": "Every gesture the player has for moving items: drags, Ctrl, Alt and Shift clicks, double-click and the item keys, and what decides each one.", "h": ["Dragging", "Clicks", "Keys", "When a move cannot happen", "Change the gestures", "What reaches your game", "Result"], "x": "Players move items by dragging them, by clicking with a modifier and by pressing a key over them. With an Inventory component on the pawn, every gesture on this page works out of the box. This page lists them all and says what decides whether each one goes through. Dragging Press on a tile and move the mouse to pick it up. A ghost the size of the item follows the pointer, held where you grabbed it, and repaints for whatever is under it. Let go somewhere that does not take it and the item goes back where it was. Onto a grid Moves the item so its top left corner lands on the cell under the ghost. The covered cells light in the valid or invalid colour while you hover. It goes through when the item stays inside the grid, overlaps nothing and the container's Accepts takes it. R while dragging Turns the item a quarter about the pointer. It lands turned. The key is the one on the shipped Rotate row. Onto a weapon tile Fits a magazine, scope or other attachment into the free slot of its kind on that weapon. Onto a worn weapon on the doll Fits the attachment the same way, onto the gun the player wears. Onto a doll slot Equips the item when the slot is the one it fits and is empty. Onto an empty container block Equips a pack, rig or secure case in that slot. Onto the inspect window Drop an attachment on one of the window's slots, or anywhere on the window, and it fits into the slot of its kind on the item being inspected. A worn slot or block onto a grid Takes the item off and puts it at that cell. Fixed items, such as the pockets and the secure case on the stash screen, cannot be dragged. A fitted attachment out of the inspect window Drag a filled slot from the window onto a grid to take the part off the weapon. Onto a workbench part slot Loads the part into a recipe. Each slot shows the item it wants and takes only that item, and only while it is empty. Between your gear and loot Moves the item from a crate or body into your gear, or back, with everything inside it or fitted to it. Loot rules can refuse it. See Containers . The raid inventory. Items drag between your gear, the loot and the doll. Clicks Ctrl-click Moves the item to where it obviously goes. If a container window is open in front, the item goes into it. Otherwise an item in the stash goes into your gear, trying the rig's pouches, then the pockets, then the pack. A bag only goes into the pack. An item in the pack, rig or pockets goes to the stash. Loot goes into your gear. A worn item comes off into the stash. Alt-click Equips the item in its slot. Shift-click Splits a stack in half. The new stack goes to the first free cell of the same grid. Double-click Opens a bag or crate in a window. Anything else opens the inspect window. Right-click Opens the item menu. See Context menu . Keys These act on the item under the pointer, or on the item whose menu is open. R Rotates the item a quarter where it lies, when it fits that way. Delete Drops the item. Worn gear has to come off first. E Takes a worn item off into the stash. When a move cannot happen A refused move tells the player why. If the target has no room, a NO ROOM toast says \"Nothing fits in\" and names the container. If a container's accept rule refuses the item, a REFUSED toast shows its refusal text. If a loot rule keeps the item on a body, a toast with the item's name says why. Change the gestures Every click and key above, except dragging, is a row of the item menu. Each row is an Item Action Definition data asset with a Gesture and a Key , so you change a shortcut by editing or replacing that asset. Changing the Rotate row's key also changes the key that turns an item while dragging. See Context menu . What reaches your game Each gesture raises a request with a tag under NVS.View.Request.Item , such as NVS.View.Request.Item.Move , on the NVS View Request Subsystem . The Inventory component applies it to itself and the screens redraw. You do not need to handle these requests. To react to a move, bind On Inventory Changed on the component, or bind On Request on the subsystem and check the request's tag. A game with its own inventory receives each request in Handle Request on the NVS View Item Holder interface. See Requests . The workbench. Each part slot shows the item it wants, and parts are dragged in from the stash. Result Play the demo and open the stash from the hub. Drag a KA-47 magazine onto the KA-47 rifle tile in the stash and it fits. Ctrl-click a ration in the stash and it goes into your gear. Shift-click a stack to split it, hover a tile and press R to turn it, and drag your pack off the doll onto the stash grid to take it off. Guide Context menu The rows behind every click and key. Guide Containers Grids, bags, crates and loot rules. Guide Equipment Equip slots and the doll."}, {"t": "Equipment", "u": "equipment.html", "s": "Give items an equip slot, wear them on the paper doll, show them on the character, and kit up from a saved loadout.", "h": ["Equip slots", "The doll's tiles", "Equip and unequip", "From Blueprint", "Worn gear on the character", "Kit up from a loadout", "Result"], "x": "An item is worn when its definition names an equip slot and the player puts it there. This guide covers the slots, every way to equip and unequip, how worn gear shows on the character, and how Kit up wears a whole loadout at once. The paper doll. Each slot is a tile around the figure, and the figure wears what the slots hold. Equip slots Slots are gameplay tags that ship with the plugin, under NVS.View.Slot : Head Ears Eyes Face Armour Rig Pack Secure Medical Legs Primary Sidearm Secondary Melee Watch Tag Gloves Boots Belt1 to Belt10 To make an item wearable, open its Item Definition and set Equip Slot on its type. Weapon, Armour, Container and Gear have the field. Each item fits exactly one slot, and each slot holds one item. Weapon Primary , Secondary , Sidearm or Melee . Armour Armour for a plate carrier, Head for a helmet. Container Pack , Rig or Secure . A worn container's grids appear beside the doll. See Containers . Gear Anything else worn, such as Ears for a headset, Gloves , Legs for trousers, Boots or Watch . The doll's tiles The paper doll ( W_PaperDoll ) lays its tiles out from its Tiles table, in three columns with the middle one left clear for the figure. It ships with tiles for Ears, Head, Eyes, Armour, Face, Watch (sharing a cell with Tag), Gloves, Boots, Primary, Sidearm, Secondary and Melee. Pack, Rig and Secure show as container blocks beside the grids instead. The other slots, such as Legs , Medical and the belt slots, hold items and show them on the character, but have no tile on the shipped doll. To give one a tile, add a row to Tiles in your own copy of the doll. Each row is a Slot Id (the last part of the tag, such as Legs ), a Column , a Row , a Span of one or two columns, and an optional Paired With for two slots sharing one cell. Equip and unequip All of these work out of the box when the player's pawn has an Inventory component. Alt-click a tile Equips it in its slot. Equip row The item menu's Equip · SLOT row. Greyed out when the slot is already taken. Drag onto the doll Drop the tile on its slot's tile, or on an empty container block for a pack, rig or secure case. Inspect window button The main button reads Equip · SLOT for an item that can be worn. Equipping never swaps. The slot must be the one the item's type names, and it must be empty. Take off what is there first, or use Kit up, which does swap. E Hover a worn item and press E to take it off into the stash. Ctrl-click a worn item Takes it off into the stash. Unequip row The item menu's Unequip row. Greyed out when the stash has no room. Drag off the doll Drag a filled slot or a worn block onto any grid. The item lands at that cell. Fixed items cannot come off. On the stash screen the pockets and the secure case are fixed. From Blueprint The Inventory component has the same calls, so your own game code can dress the player: Equip Item(Instance Id, Slot) Wears an entry in the slot its type names. False if the slot is wrong or taken. Unequip Item(Slot, Container) Takes off what the slot holds into the first free cell of the container, or of any base container when Container is None. Get Equipped(Slot) The entry worn in a slot, or false when it is empty. Attach Item(Instance Id, Host Instance, Slot) Fits an attachment to one of a weapon's attachment slots. See Weapons . Worn gear on the character The figure on the doll, on the character screen and in the world wears what the player has equipped. Where each item sits while worn is set on its Item Definition under Type . Only the wearable types have these fields: Weapon, Armour, Gear and Container. Worn On The bone or socket of the body its Look mesh hangs from, such as Head for a helmet, spine_03 for a rifle on the back or thigh_r for the SMG. Worn Offset Location, rotation and scale relative to that bone. Leave Worn On as None and the item shows nothing on the body, with one exception. A Gear, Armour or Container item whose Look has only a Skeletal Mesh follows the body's pose like clothing. A weapon always needs a bone. Gear made of several meshes works best as one mesh with a material slot for each colour. The demo's plate carrier is one mesh on spine_03 . Its cargo trousers, boots and gloves are Ranger skeletal meshes with no Worn On , so they follow the body. See Character looks . A weapon worn on a bone, such as a rifle slung across the back, brings whatever is fitted to it, such as a scope or a magazine. Getting gear to sit right on a bone takes a few tries. Placing weapon parts covers worn offsets and attachment slots step by step. To dress the character in your level with the same gear, see Character looks . For the gun in the player's hands, Show Held Item on NVS View Look Library puts a held entry, with everything fitted to it, on a static mesh component. Kit up from a loadout A Loadout Definition is a named kit: what is worn, what is fitted to the guns and what is packed where. The loadout screen lists every one. Create the asset Make a Data Asset of class Loadout Definition . Set Display Name and Description , the line under the name in the library. Fill Items Each row is a starting item row: Equip Slot to wear it, Fitted To Slot to fit it to a worn gun, or Packed In Slot and Packed Grid to pack it into a worn bag or a rig pouch. See Starting items . Validate The asset flags packed items with nothing worn to go in, and items too big for the grid they name. When the player presses Kit up , the Inventory component wears, fits and packs the kit from what the player owns in the stash. Worn pieces go on first. Whatever was in a slot goes back to the stash to make room. A toast then says \"Everything came out of your stash.\" or how many pieces were not there. The loadout library. Kit up wears the chosen kit from the stash. From Blueprint, call Kit Up on the Inventory component with a Loadout Definition. It returns how many pieces were missing. Result Give a helmet definition Equip Slot NVS.View.Slot.Head and Worn On set to the Head bone, and put one"}, {"t": "Inspect", "u": "inspect.html", "s": "What the inspect window shows, where each line comes from in the Item Definition, and how the preview turns, zooms and frames the item.", "h": ["How it opens", "What the sheet shows", "Fitting parts from the sheet", "The picture", "How it frames and turns the item", "Preview profiles", "Result"], "x": "The inspect window is an item's full sheet: a 3D view the player can turn and zoom, its description and numbers, its attachment slots and the actions it allows. Everything on it is read from the item's Item Definition, so there is nothing to set up beyond the item itself. The inspect window, open over the stash. How it opens Double-click an item tile, a filled doll slot or a worn container block. An item that opens a container opens that instead, and offers Inspect in its menu. Inspect in the item menu. From C++ , with OpenInspect on the screen that owns the item. It takes an FNVSViewItemSubject built the way a tile builds one. The window can be moved and resized, nothing dims behind it, and inspecting the same item again brings its window to the front. What the sheet shows Tag, name and where The tile's word and the item's Display Name , then where it is: EQUIPPED with the slot, IN THE STASH, ON THE SHELF, IN THE WARDROBE, CREW KIT · READ ONLY or IN A CONTAINER. What it takes up The Footprint in cells. Description The item's Description . Weight The item's Weight , such as 3.4 KG . Value The item's Value , in the setup's money format. Fits slot The slot from the type's Equip Slot , or NOT EQUIPPABLE. Stack The count, such as ×3, or SINGLE. Condition The type's word, such as WEAPON or AMMO, with a note that condition is checked on extraction. Slots A weapon's attachment slots, each empty or showing the part fitted in it. Hidden for items with none. Actions A main button, Rotate and Drop. The main button is Unequip for a worn item, Equip · SLOT for an item that can be worn (enabled when the slot is free), and Sell with the value for anything else worth something. Rotate is enabled for an item lying in a grid, and the window stays open to show the new footprint. A teammate's gear and a wardrobe piece hide the actions and show a note that the sheet is view only. Fitting parts from the sheet Drag an attachment onto one of the slots, or anywhere on the window, and it fits into the free slot of its kind. Drag a fitted part out of its slot onto a grid to take it off. See Weapons for attachment slots. The picture The window uses the first of these that exists: The item's Icon When the definition sets Icon , that texture is shown. A live 3D view When the definition has a Look with a base, the item is built live in the window so the player can turn it. Fitted parts show on it. A rendered icon If the live view is not available, a captured picture of the look. The tag glyph The tile's outline glyph, tinted. A worn item is looked up by the item in the slot, so inspecting a slot shows what is in it. How it frames and turns the item The live view opens at the item's icon angle, so it looks the way its tile does. That angle is yaw -35 and pitch -20 unless the item's look overrides it. The camera fits the mesh's bounds. Drag turns the item, half a degree per pixel. Scroll zooms between 0.8 and 2.0. Showing a different item resets the turn and the zoom. To change the starting angle for one item, open its Item Definition, expand Look > Framing , tick Override and set: Yaw, Pitch The angle in degrees. Yaw -90 shows the item side on. Zoom Above 1 fills more of the frame. Offset A shift as a fraction of the frame, so it works at every size. The same framing is used for the item's tile pictures, so the tile and the window stay consistent. Preview profiles How a 3D view behaves in one place is a Preview Profile data asset. The plugin ships four in Content/Render . Only the character screen animates its figure, and none of them swoops the camera. PP_Inspect The inspect window. Still pose, turn and zoom on, zoom up to 2.0, redraws only when the item changes. PP_Doll The paper doll's figure. Still pose, no turning, redraws live. PP_Character The character screen. Animated idle, turn on, redraws live. PP_Portrait Crew portraits. Still and fixed, redraws on change. The fields you can change on a profile: Pose Animated Idle loops the look's Character Preview Animation. Static holds its first frame. Framing, Framing Zone Full Body, Upper Body, or Zone with a body zone such as head or chest. Rotate, Rotate Button, Degrees Per Pixel Whether dragging turns the subject, with which mouse button, and how fast. Ease Back To Front, Ease Back Speed After a drag, the subject turns back to face the camera. Zoom, Min Zoom, Max Zoom, Zoom Step Whether scrolling zooms, its range and its step. Focus Hovering a doll slot eases the camera to that slot's body zone. Gamepad Turn Axis, Gamepad Tilt Axis, Gamepad Degrees Per Second, Gamepad Dead Zone Turning with a stick. Redraw Live captures every frame while animating or dragged. On Change captures only when the look changes, so a still preview costs nothing per frame. To change the inspect window's behaviour, duplicate PP_Inspect into your own folder, edit the copy, and set it as Profile on the ItemPreview widget in your own copy of W_InspectWindow . Plugin assets are replaced when you update the plugin. Result Double-click a weapon in your stash. The inspect window opens with the weapon at its icon angle. Drag to turn it and scroll to zoom. The sheet shows your description, weight and value, and dropping a scope on the window fits it to the gun in front of you. Guide Items The definition every line of the sheet comes from. Guide Weapons Attachment slots the sheet shows. Systems Rendering Icons and live previews."}, {"t": "Character looks", "u": "character-looks.html", "s": "Give items a look, build the player's figure from Wardrobe Piece data assets, show worn gear on it, and dress the character in your level the same way.", "h": ["Where the pictures come from", "An item's look", "The body figure's look", "Worn gear on the body", "The wardrobe", "How the figure is built", "Clothing that hides the skin", "Try on, buy and wear", "The example characters", "Dress your in-game character", "Result"], "x": "Blackline draws item pictures, the player's figure and the wardrobe from data you fill in the Details panel. Items carry a Look and their type says how they are worn, and the figure is built from Wardrobe Piece Definition assets. This guide shows how each part works and how to dress the character in your level with the same outfit. Where the pictures come from Item tiles and the inspect window render the item's Look from its Item Definition. The figure on the doll, the character screen and the health screen is built from the wardrobe pieces the player wears, with their equipped items on top. Your level gets the same figure through Dress Character , so the character in the world wears what the menus show. Every mesh, material and animation in a look is a soft reference. Nothing loads until something shows it. An item's look Open an Item Definition and expand Look . It names what draws the item on tiles, in the inspect window and on the body while it is worn. Static Mesh The item's mesh. Most items use this one. Skeletal Mesh Used when Static Mesh is empty. Clothing such as trousers uses one so it can follow the body. Pickup Actor An actor shown instead of the mesh, such as a fully built rifle Blueprint. Its meshes are framed and lit like any other. Framing Overrides the automatic camera angle, zoom and offset. See Inspect . Gear made of several pieces works best as one mesh with a material slot for each colour. The demo's gear and small items, such as the plate carrier, the helmet, the med kit and the fuel can, are each built that way. Fitted attachments show on the item's picture everywhere it is drawn, on the tile, in the inspect window and on the gun slung on the character. Where each one sits is set on the weapon's attachment slot. See Placing weapon parts . The body figure's look A body piece's Figure is the base every wardrobe piece is added to, so it has more fields than an item's look. You set it on a Wardrobe Piece Definition, as described in The wardrobe . A figure is a base plus a list of parts. Static Base / Skeletal Base / Actor Base The base. Set one. Actor Base spawns an actor instead of a mesh. A figure without a base draws nothing. Base Offset A transform applied to the base, to turn a model to face the camera or to scale it. Base Material Replaces the base's first material. Base Scalars Scalar parameters set on the base's material. Parts Extra meshes added to the base. See the part fields below. Character Preview Animation The animation the character preview loops. A still preview holds its first frame. Framing Overrides the automatic camera angle, zoom and offset. When a preview turns to a part of the body, it looks for the standard bone names head , spine_03 , upperarm_l , thigh_l and calf_l , or the matching part of the built-in mannequin of shapes. A skeleton with those names needs nothing more. Each part in a figure has these fields: Part Name A name for the part. The live preview rebuilds only parts whose name or content changed. Static Mesh / Skeletal Mesh The part's mesh. A skeletal part on a skeletal base follows the base's pose. Socket The socket or bone to attach to. None attaches at the base's root. Offset A transform relative to the socket. Tint A colour applied through the engine's basic shape material. Leave it transparent to keep the mesh's own material. Material Replaces the part's first material, for a colourway of the same mesh. Equipment Marks worn or carried gear, as opposed to the body and clothing. Worn gear on the body An equipped item shows its Look on the body in one of two ways. Both are set on the Item Definition under Type , and only the wearable types have the fields: Weapon, Armour, Gear and Container. Worn items are added on top of the wardrobe, and they come and go as the player equips and unequips. Rigid gear such as a helmet or a slung rifle: set Worn On to a bone or socket and Worn Offset to where it sits. The item's Look mesh hangs there. The demo's helmet sits on Head , its rifles and knife on spine_03 and its SMG on thigh_r . Clothing such as trousers, boots or gloves: give the item a Look with only a Skeletal Mesh and leave Worn On as None. The mesh follows the body's pose. This works for Gear, Armour and Container items. A weapon always needs a bone. The demo's cargo trousers, boots and gloves use the CC0 Ranger legs, boots and arms meshes this way. Any other item with Worn On left as None shows nothing on the body. Whatever is fitted to a weapon, such as a scope or a magazine, rides along with it when it is slung. Worn clothing is drawn on top of the wardrobe and does not hide the wardrobe piece underneath. If both show, change the wardrobe outfit to suit, for example a piece in that slot that sits under the clothing, or none. See Equipment for how worn gear behaves, and Placing weapon parts for getting offsets right. The wardrobe The character screen shows the figure on one side and a filterable grid of wardrobe pieces on the other. Each piece is a Wardrobe Piece Definition data asset. Create them the same way as items, as Data Assets of class Wardrobe Piece Definition . The plugin finds them by itself. The character screen. The figure wears the chosen pieces and the worn strip sits under it. Display Name The piece's name. Category The wardrobe filter it is listed under, such as Jacket. Slot The wardrobe slot it is worn in, such as Top , Legs , Feet , Head , Hair or Body . Wearing it takes off whatever else is in that slot. These are names you choose, separate from the equip slots. Focus Zone Where the camera frames while the piece is chosen: head, chest, arms, legs or feet. None frames the whole figure. Source Where it comes from, such as Trader · Vulture . Price Zero means the player owns it from the start. Worn At Start Worn by a new player. One per slot. Figure Set only on a body piece . This is the base figure every other piece is added to: a Skeletal Base , a Character Preview Animation and any always-on parts such as eyes"}, {"t": "Market and trading", "u": "market-and-trading.html", "s": "Make traders as data assets, buy and sell with category chips, run trader jobs and the player market, and see where the cash comes from.", "h": ["Cash", "Money format", "Traders are data assets", "The trade screen", "Category chips", "Buying", "Selling", "Trader jobs", "The market", "The market's rules", "What the market raises", "Answer the requests yourself", "Result"], "x": "Blackline ships three screens for moving money and goods: the trader roster, the trade screen for one trader, and a player market with auctions. Traders are data assets, and with an Inventory component on the player's pawn, buying, selling, barter and trader jobs work with no request code at all. Cash Every screen that shows or spends money shares one session wallet, NVSViewCash . Buying from a trader, Heal all, station upgrades and wardrobe purchases spend it. Selling to a trader and handing in a task's reward add to it. The wallet starts from the Wallet.OnHand meter of the wallet source the first time anything asks. The demo sets that figure in data: Make the wallet asset Create a data asset of class NVS View Wallet , called DA_Wallet in the demo, and give it a meter with the id Wallet.OnHand and a value of 12480. Plug it in Create a Screen Source Definition with Replaces set to NVS View Wallet and Asset set to DA_Wallet . The same wallet also answers Wallet.StashValue , worked out from what the player's stash holds, which the trade screen shows beside the cash. The cash lasts for the play session. It is not written to a save game, and there is no Blueprint node to read or set it. A game that keeps its own money answers the requests itself, as shown at the end of this page. Money format Every price on screen goes through NVSViewMarket::FormatMoney , which reads three fields of your View Setup Definition : Currency Symbol Defaults to € . Symbol After Amount Puts the symbol after the number with a space, as in 5,200 kr . Thousands Separator Defaults to a comma. Write whole amounts. The screens read money as whole units. Traders are data assets Make one Trader Definition per trader. The roster and the trade screen pick it up with no configuration, ordered by Sort Order . Display Name, Trade, Location, Blurb The name, what they deal in such as Guns & powder, where they are, and a paragraph about them. Wants What they buy, shown on the card, such as WEAPONS · AMMO . Loyalty, Max Loyalty The player's standing at the start and the highest it goes. The standing bar fills to Loyalty over Max Loyalty. Buy Rate The share of an item's value they pay when the player sells, from 0 to 1. Accent The colour role their card and selection use. Shelf What they sell. Each entry has an Item , a Stock count, a Price (zero sells at the item's value) and an optional Barter list of items and counts they take instead of money. The demo's three traders, from make_content.py : Trader Trade Loyalty Buy Rate Barter example Brigitte Salvage & supply 2 0.7 Pump house key for 6 copper spools Oskar Guns & powder 3 0.62 4x scope for 2 fuel cans Mira Field medicine 1 0.78 Plate carrier for 2 field med kits The trader roster. Each card is one Trader Definition. Choosing a card opens the screen set in the roster's Counter Destination , which is the trade screen, showing that trader. The trade screen The trade screen ( W_TradeScreen ) shows the trader's shelf on the left, a deal column with Buy and Sell tabs in the middle, and the player's stash on the right. A second view lists the trader's jobs. The trade screen with a shelf item picked and the buy tab open. Category chips The chips beside the shelf and the stash are All plus one chip per Item Category Definition , in sort order. A category takes every item whose tags carry its Tag or a tag below it, so a category with Item.Weapon holds Item.Weapon.Rifle . Set Icon on the category to show an icon on its chip instead of its name. Buying The player picks a shelf item, sets a quantity and presses Buy. The screen raises Item.Buy . The player's Inventory component answers it. It charges the shelf price times the quantity from the cash, puts the items in the stash, and refunds any that did not fit. When the cash is short, nothing changes. A shelf entry with a Barter list is barter only. The deal column shows the terms instead of a quantity, and the button raises Trade.Swap . The inventory checks the player holds every item asked for, takes them, and hands over the item. Selling The player drags items from the stash onto the counter tray. The tray total is each item's value times the trader's Buy Rate. Sell it all raises one Item.Sell per item, carrying that figure as Amount . The inventory removes the item and adds the amount to the cash. Items left on the tray go back to the stash when the screen closes. Trader jobs A Task Definition whose Giver is this trader shows in the jobs view. The action button takes a job that reads ON OFFER and hands in one that reads READY TO HAND IN . Handing in takes the items the task asks for out of the player's inventory, pays the task's Reward Cash once, and the job reads TURNED IN . The task log follows the same state. Tasks and loadouts covers the task fields. The market The market ( W_MarketScreen ) is a player market with buyouts and auctions. It has three views: browse, the player's own listings, and the watchlist. A detail panel shows a listing's price against the average, the seller, recent bids and the bid controls. A sell box beside the stash lists one item at a time. The market. The header shows cash, escrow, listing slots and money to collect. Its built-in source, UNVSViewMarketBoard , runs a market offline. It fills the catalogue from every Item Definition with a Value , groups listings by your item categories, and opens a few listings per item under the names in Seller Names , which comes filled with eight names. Empty that list and the board opens with no listings. To change these fields, make a data asset of class NVS View Market Board and plug it in with a Screen Source Definition. Its clock runs in real time from the market's first open this session. Rivals outbid the player after Rival Delay seconds, a fairly priced buyout of the player's sells after Sell After seconds, and the player's auctions take a bid every Bid Every seconds up to Max Bids . When it has no cash of its own it uses the session cash. It is there to show the m"}, {"t": "The raid loop", "u": "raid-loop.html", "s": "Run a raid from the lobby to the debrief: pick a map, deploy through the queue, loot crates and bodies, extract or go missing in action.", "h": ["The loop at a glance", "Make the raid maps", "The lobby and the deploy queue", "Load the level on Raid.Deploy", "In the raid", "Crates and bodies", "Extract", "Missing in action", "The debrief", "Limits", "Result"], "x": "Blackline runs the whole raid loop: the lobby, the location screen, the deploy queue, the raid HUD, looting, the extract bar and the debrief. Your game loads the level and says when the raid ends. This guide wires that up in Blueprint, the way the Blackline Demo does. The loop at a glance The player opens the lobby ( W_PartyLobby ). The map button names the chosen raid map. The location screen ( W_InsertionSelect ) shows one point per raid map. Picking a point calls Set Selected Raid Map . Move out starts the deploy queue: searching, match found, countdown. The queue pushes the raid HUD, calls Begin Raid and raises Raid.Deploy . Your game loads the map's level. Interacting with a crate or a body calls Open Loot Screen with that actor. Standing in an exit fills the HUD's extract bar. At the end your game calls End Raid with Extracted. If the raid clock runs out, the raid ends as missing in action and everything carried is lost except the secure case. Your game opens the debrief ( W_RaidSummary ), which shows the result, the haul and the time. Make the raid maps Each raid map is a Raid Map Definition data asset. The lobby, the location screen, the raid HUD's exits and the raid clock all read it. Display Name, Conditions The map's name and a line such as DUSK · DRY · 15 MIN RAID . Region Position Where its point sits on the location screen, from 0 to 1 across and down, over the Region Image of your View Setup Definition. Briefing, Stats What the location screen says when the point is picked, and the figures under it. Empty uses the first insertion point's. Map Image The picture of the map itself, drawn under its markers. Insertions, Exfils, Exfil Rule Start points and exits with their positions on the map image. An exfil has a Cost such as Free, can be shut with a reason, and the rule line sits under the exit list on the HUD. Level The level your game loads when the player deploys. Raid Seconds How long the raid lasts before the player is missing in action. Default 1800. Zero means no clock. The demo has two maps, Silt Yards and Pump Station, each with one insertion point, one exit and a Raid Seconds of 900. Until the player picks one, the first map by Sort Order is the selected map. The location screen. Each point is a Raid Map Definition. The lobby and the deploy queue The lobby. Move out starts the queue, and the map button opens the location screen. The lobby's map button opens the screen set in its Insertion Destination . Leave that unset to hide the button. The location screen has its own queue button, so the player can deploy from either screen. Pressing Move out starts the deploy queue ( UNVSViewDeployQueueSubsystem ). It lives on the local player, so it keeps running when the lobby closes. It runs three phases, timed by Queue Timings on the screen that started it: Phase Default What the player sees Searching 9 s The queue button shows the search. Pressing it again cancels. Found 3 s The match found screen, and the crew cards lock in. It can no longer be cancelled. Countdown 6 s The drop countdown. The screens it pushes at each phase are the screen's Match Found Overlay , Countdown Overlay and Raid Destination . When the countdown ends, the queue pushes the raid HUD, calls Begin Raid on the NVS View Raid Session Subsystem with the selected map, and raises the request NVS.View.Request.Raid.Deploy with the map's name as Subject Id . Load the level on Raid.Deploy Bind On Request In your player controller, get the NVSViewRequestSubsystem and bind On Request . Find the map When Request Id is NVS.View.Request.Raid.Deploy , call Get Raid Map Definition with the request's Subject Id . Load its level Load the definition's Level . The demo streams it in with Load Level Instance by Object Reference , moves the pawn to the level's start point, and adds the raid input mapping. In the raid The raid HUD. The navigation, vitals and crew groups stay hidden until revealed. The raid session keeps the clock. In Blueprint, get the subsystem from the player controller and read: Is In Raid True between Begin Raid and End Raid. Get Seconds Left Until the map's clock runs out. Zero with no raid or no clock. Get Seconds In Raid Since the raid began. After it ends, how long it lasted. Get Map Id The map of the current or last raid. The clock uses world time, so pausing the game stops it. The demo's BPC_RaidClock component turns it into the HUD's two clocks, meters Raid.Clock and Raid.Exfil , the same way BPC_Vitals feeds the vitals (see Health and vitals ). Crates and bodies Anything the player can loot is an actor with an Inventory component. Set these on it: Base Containers Its grids. The demo's crate has one 6 by 4 grid called Crate . Its body has a 4 by 1 Pockets grid. Starting Items What is inside, and for a body what it wears. A body can wear a rig and a pack with items packed in them. Display Name The heading of the loot pane, such as SUPPLY CRATE or DEAD OPERATOR . Needs Search Hides each container's contents behind a search veil until the player searches it. The time comes from the setup's Search Base Seconds and Search Seconds Per Cell , and a body searches faster by Search Body Factor . Applies Loot Rules Makes the items' loot rules count, as for a body. Clothes with the Stays On Body rule cannot be taken, and a secure case with the Sealed rule cannot be opened. When the player interacts with it, call Open Loot Screen with the player controller and the actor. The raid inventory opens with the player's gear on one side and the loot on the other. A body's worn gear counts as loot, so the player takes it into their own gear. The loot pane has Crate and Body tabs. Calling it again while the screen is open changes only the loot side. Call it with no actor to open the player's own gear alone, and the loot pane is hidden. The raid inventory. The loot pane's heading names what is being looted. The demo's character traces forward when F is pressed. A loose pickup goes in with Pick Up Item , which fills the "}, {"t": "Health and vitals", "u": "health-and-vitals.html", "s": "Set up body zones and treatments, price Heal all, feed food and water, read load tiers, and drive the raid HUD's vitals from a Blueprint component.", "h": ["Body zones", "Treatments", "Heal all", "Hurt the body from Blueprint", "Food and water", "Load tiers", "The raid HUD groups", "Walkthrough of the demo's BPC_Vitals", "Its variables", "How it is built", "Result"], "x": "The health screen treats body zones with meds from the stash, food and water come from what the player eats, and the load tiers say how fast a player can move. This guide sets each one up in data and then walks through the demo's BPC_Vitals Blueprint, which drives food, water and sprinting on the raid HUD. Body zones The health screen ( W_HealthScreen ) reads the body from UNVSViewBodyZones . It lists zones in a fixed order, centre three first, then the left six, then the right six, so the screen can deal them into columns. To author your own body, make a data asset of class NVS View Body Zones and plug it in with a Screen Source Definition whose Replaces is NVS View Body Zones . The demo's are DA_PlayerBody and PDA_PlayerBodySource . Each zone is one entry in Zones and one record in Records with the same id: Record Id The zone's id, such as Zone.Chest . Hit Points, Max Hit Points Where the zone starts and its total. The demo's chest starts at 62 of 85. Conditions One chip per entry, such as BANDAGED . Empty uses the record's status text, such as LIGHT BLEED , as a single chip. A zone's colour follows its share of hit points: red at zero, the accent colour under Low Fraction (0.35), yellow under Hurt Fraction (0.75), and green above. Blackline does not simulate wounds. A condition such as a bleed or a fracture is a line of text you author, and nothing drains hit points over time unless your game calls Hurt Body . The health screen. Pick a zone, then click a med in the stash beside it. Treatments Which meds treat which zone comes from UNVSViewZoneTreatments , set up the same way with a data asset and a Screen Source Definition. Each entry in Zone Fixes has a Zone Id , the Meds (Item Definitions) that treat it, and the Fixes rows shown for a zone that lists no meds. The rows say IN THE STASH or NONE IN THE STASH for each med, so the player knows what they have. A med is an Item Definition with the Consumable type. Its Heals field is the hit points it gives back to a zone. A med with Heals at zero still treats what is wrong, as a bandage stops a bleed. The demo's field med kit heals 60. When the player picks a zone and clicks a med in the stash, the screen checks three things: the item is a consumable, the zone needs work, and the zone lists that med. It then raises Item.Use with the zone as Subject Id . The Inventory component uses up one of the med, and the zone gets the med's hit points, loses its conditions and reads TREATED until it is whole. A zone at zero hit points needs a med whose Heals is above zero. Heal all The Heal all button treats every zone and fills it for cash. Its price is: Heal Price Per Point (10 by default) for every missing hit point, plus Treatment Price (150 by default) for every zone with something nobody has treated. The button shows the price, and reads Not enough cash and stays disabled when the session cash is short. Pressing it raises Health.HealAll , and the body zones source spends the cash and heals. Both prices are fields on the body zones asset. Hurt the body from Blueprint All of these are on NVS View Screen Library : Get Body Health The player's health out of 100, from the hit points of every zone. Treating and healing raise it. Hurt Body (Hit Points) Takes hit points off the body, shared across the zones by their size. A zone never drops below 1 this way, so hunger wears the player down without blacking out a limb. Treatment and damage last for the play session. They are not written to a save game. Food and water A Consumable's Food and Water fields are what it gives back, out of 100. The demo's ration pack gives 30 food and its water bottle 40 water. When the player uses one from a grid, the Inventory component removes it and adds its food and water to the session's total. Get Nourishment returns the food and water eaten and drunk this session. Blackline keeps no food or water figure of its own, so add these to the values your game tracks. The demo's BPC_Vitals shows how. Load tiers The Load Tiers of your View Setup Definition name the gait each weight allows, fastest first, each with the kilograms it ends at. The demo uses SPRINT up to 20 kg, JOG to 28 and WALK to 36. The load card draws the track and shows what dropping weight would gain. Get Carried Weight (Player) Everything the player's pawn wears and carries, in kilograms. The stash does not count. Get Load Tier (Player) The tier that weight falls in, counting from 0 for the lightest. -1 is over the last tier's limit. Blackline does not move the character. Your movement code reads the tier and sets its own speed. The raid HUD groups The raid HUD's navigation, vitals and crew groups start hidden. Reveal Hud Group shows or hides one: Nav The compass, the tracked task and the exits. Vitals Body, limbs, conditions and weapon. Crew The crew. It returns false when no raid HUD is open. The HUD reads no keys itself, so your game decides when each group shows. Walkthrough of the demo's BPC_Vitals BPC_Vitals is a Blueprint Actor Component on the demo's player controller. It feeds the HUD's vitals meters, runs food and water down during a raid, and handles sprinting. It is an ordinary Blueprint graph you can open and copy. The raid HUD. BPC_Vitals reveals the vitals while the player is out of breath. Its variables Variable Default What it does Food, Water 62, 38 The current values, out of 100. Wind 100 Breath for sprinting, out of 100. Carry Limit Kg 36 Matches the last load tier. Food Per Minute, Water Per Minute 2, 3 Lost each minute in a raid. Sprint Wind Per Second 18 Wind spent each second of sprinting with nothing carried. A full load doubles it. Wind Back Per Second 10 Wind regained each second while not sprinting. Hunger Damage Per Second 0.5 Hit points lost each second while food or water is empty. Walk Speed, Sprint Speed 420, 680 The character's speeds. Loaded Speed 260 The speed from the third tier, WALK in the demo. Overloaded Speed 140 The speed over the last tier's limit. How it is built St"}, {"t": "Hideout and crafting", "u": "hideout-and-crafting.html", "s": "Make hideout stations as data assets, craft from parts dropped into recipe slots, collect finished crafts, upgrade benches and run the generator.", "h": ["Make a station", "The hideout screen", "Craft on the workbench", "Upgrade a station", "The generator", "Result"], "x": "The hideout is a set of stations, each a data asset with levels and recipes. The player drags parts from the stash into a recipe's slots, starts the craft, and collects the result when the timer runs out. This guide makes a station, runs a craft through the workbench and covers upgrades and the generator. Make a station Create a Station Definition data asset per station. The hideout, the workbench screen and the generator read them with no configuration. Display Name, Description Shown on the station's card and at the top of its bench. Icon The art its card shows, by name. Power Draw The kilowatts it pulls from the generator while a craft runs. Start Level The level a new player has. Zero means it is not built yet. Levels One entry per level, the first being the build itself. Each has a Gain line, a cash Cost , a Parts list and a Downtime line. See Upgrade a station . Recipes What the station makes, described below. Each recipe has these fields: Id Unique within the station. Requests about the recipe carry it. Output, Output Count The Item Definition it makes and how many. Display Name, Description Empty uses the output item's. Craft Time How long it takes, written as text such as 22 m , 40 s or 1 h 35 m . Required Level The station level it needs. Requirement An extra line such as LOYALTY 3 . While it is set the recipe stays locked, so leave it empty on any recipe the player should be able to make. Parts The items it needs, each an Item Definition and a count. The demo's workbench, from make_content.py : Recipe Makes Time Parts Level AR4Mag 3 AR-4 magazines 30 s 2 copper spools, 1 fuel can 1 Scope 4x scope 45 s 4 copper spools 1 KA47 KA-47 rifle 1 m 30 s 6 copper spools, 1 fuel can, 1 KA-47 magazine 3 It starts at level 2 of 4 and draws 1.2 kW. Its levels cost 6,000, then 9,000, then 18,000 and 2 copper spools, then 32,000, 4 copper spools and a KA-47 magazine. The hideout screen The hideout ( W_Hideout ) shows every station as a card under a generator header. Cards are sorted by what needs the player: ready to collect first, then running, idle, and not built. Choosing a card opens that station's bench. The hideout. The action bar's button collects everything that is ready. The action bar's button reads Collect all · 2 when two stations have finished crafts, and Nothing to collect otherwise. Pressing it raises Station.Collect for each ready station. Craft on the workbench The bench screen ( W_BenchScreen ) shows one station: its recipes, the chosen recipe's part slots, the stash beside them, and in Upgrade mode the station's levels. The workbench. Each part slot shows the item it wants. The player picks a recipe. Each part slot shows a picture of the exact item it needs, its category and how many, and reads EMPTY . The player drags the item from the stash onto its slot. The screen raises Bench.LoadPart , and the Inventory component moves as many as the slot still needs out of the stash and onto the bench. A slot part way there reads, for example, 1 / 2 . Once every slot is full the button reads Start craft . Pressing it raises Bench.Start and the timer begins. The parts are used up. If the generator is out of fuel, the bench reads NO POWER , the button reads No power , and nothing starts. The bench reads ON THE BENCH . The timer keeps running while the player is in a raid or on another screen, as long as the generator has fuel. If the fuel runs out, the bench reads NO POWER and the timer waits until you add more. When the timer runs out the bench reads READY and the button Collect it . Collecting puts the output in the stash. Nothing comes off the bench until the stash has room. A recipe above the station's level, or with a Requirement , reads LOCKED . Loaded parts, running crafts and station levels last for the play session. They are not written to a save game. Upgrade a station Each entry in a station's Levels is one upgrade. Fill these on each level: Gain What reaching the level gives, such as Faster jobs and the tier 2 recipes . Cost The cash the upgrade takes from the session wallet. Zero costs nothing. Parts The items the player loads before paying, each an Item Definition and a count, the same as a recipe's parts. The upgrade uses them up. Leave the list empty for an upgrade that costs only cash. Downtime Text such as 1 h 30 m , shown on the next level. Kilowatts, Fuel Slots Used only on the generator. See The generator . The bench's Upgrade tab lists the levels as INSTALLED , NEXT or LATER . The next level shows its gain, cash cost, part count and downtime, with one drop slot per part under them. Each slot shows the item it wants and how many. The generator screen's Upgrade tab works the same way. The player drags each item from the stash onto its slot. The screen raises Bench.LoadPart , and the Inventory component moves as many as the slot still needs out of the stash. Until every slot is full, the button reads Load the parts · 1 / 2 and does nothing. With every part loaded, the button reads Upgrade · €18,000 , or Not enough cash · €18,000 while the wallet is short. Pressing Upgrade raises Bench.Upgrade with the station as its subject. The cash is spent, the loaded parts are used up, and the next level is installed at once. Recipes that need the new level unlock. Each upgrade has an id made from the station asset's name and the level's index in Levels , such as PDA_Workbench.Upgrade2 for the demo workbench's third level. Its part slots are named like a recipe's, PDA_Workbench.Upgrade2.Part0 and so on. UNVSViewRecipePartSlots publishes them under ContextForRecipe of the upgrade id, so a screen of your own can show the same slots. An upgraded bench is down for its level's Downtime , such as 1 m 30 s . The level is installed at once, but the bench starts no craft until it is back. The bench screen and its hideout card read UPGRADING with the time left. A craft already running carries on. The generator never goes down, because the whole hideout would lose power. Parts loaded for an"}, {"t": "Tasks and loadouts", "u": "tasks-and-loadouts.html", "s": "Write trader tasks as data assets, take and hand them in, follow them in the task log, and kit the player up from saved loadouts.", "h": ["Write a task", "Steps your game reports", "Take and hand in", "The task log", "Loadouts", "Kit up", "Saved kits", "Result"], "x": "Tasks are jobs a trader gives, such as bringing back an item for a reward. Loadouts are named kits the player can put on in one click. Both are data assets, and with an Inventory component on the player's pawn both work with no code. Write a task Create a Task Definition data asset per task. It shows among its giver's jobs on the trade screen and in the task log. Title, Description The task's name and its paragraph. Giver The Trader Definition that offers it. A task with no giver is listed under the settlement and is not offered by any trader. Requirement A line shown before the player takes it, such as LOYALTY 2 . It is shown only, and does not stop the player taking the task. Objectives The steps. Each has Text , and optionally an Item and a Count . Reward Cash, Reward Xp What handing in pays and the experience shown. Reward Lines, Unlocks, Fails If Further lines for the task sheet, such as Brigitte +2 standing . Empty Fails If reads as no time limit. A step that names an item is met when the player's inventory holds Count of it, stash included. Steps are met in order, and a closing step with no item, such as Hand it to Brigitte , is the hand in itself. A task is ready once every item it asks for is in hand. The demo's Black box task, given by Brigitte: Field Value Requirement LOYALTY 2 Objectives Find the flight recorder in the tail section (Flight recorder ×1), then Hand it to Brigitte at the yard Reward 8,400 cash and 1,200 XP Reward Lines Brigitte +2 standing Unlocks Workbench tier 2, Task: Quiet as you can Fails If You die carrying the recorder Blackline pays only the cash. The experience, reward lines, unlocks and fail conditions are text on the task sheet for your game to act on. Steps your game reports A step with no Item is something the player does in the game, such as surviving a run. Blackline cannot see that happen, so your game reports it with Advance Task Step (Task Id, Step Index, Amount). The step is done once it reaches its Count , and the task log shows progress such as 1 / 2 . Steps count from 0. Only a task the player has taken moves. Get Task Step Progress reads how far a step has got. The last step, when it has no item, is the hand-in itself and needs no report. The demo's Quiet as you can works this way: its extract event calls Advance Task Step for step 0 each time the player gets out of Silt Yards, and after two runs the job reads READY TO HAND IN . Take and hand in The player deals with tasks in the jobs view of the giver's trade screen. A job moves through these states: The job reads ON OFFER . The action button raises Job.Take . Once taken it reads IN PROGRESS , with a progress meter for its counted item, such as 0 / 1. When the player holds every item it asks for, it reads READY TO HAND IN . The button now raises Job.HandIn . Handing in takes the items out of the player's inventory, pays Reward Cash once, and the job reads TURNED IN . A job taken while the items are already in hand is ready at once. Every screen that shows the task hears the request, and it is applied only once. Task state lasts for the play session and is not written to a save game. The task log The task log ( W_TaskLog ) lists every task with its state: IN PROGRESS once taken, READY whenever the items it asks for are in hand, and HANDED IN at the end. Choosing one shows its steps, each DONE , active with its count such as 0 / 3 , or LOCKED , and its sheet with the giver, reward, unlocks and fail conditions. The task log. The sheet's columns come from the task's reward, unlock and fail lines. Two buttons act on the task in focus: Track Raises Task.Track . The task becomes the tracked one, which the raid HUD's navigation group shows with its next step. Show on map Raises Task.ShowOnMap . Blackline does not answer it, so your game decides what the map shows. Handing in happens at the trader, not in the log. Loadouts A Loadout Definition is a named kit. The loadout library lists every one in sort order. Display Name, Description The kit's name and the line under it. Items What the kit holds. Each item has a Count and says where it goes. Where each item goes is set by the first of these that applies: Equip Slot Worn in that slot, such as NVS.View.Slot.Primary . Fitted To Slot Fitted to the weapon the kit wears in that slot, such as a magazine in the primary. Packed In Slot, Packed Grid Packed inside what the kit wears in that slot, such as NVS.View.Slot.Pack . Packed Grid picks a rig's pouch, counted from zero. Container A base container of the inventory, such as Stash . None takes the first with room. The editor flags a packed item with nothing worn to go in, or one too big for the grid it names. The demo's Scav run wears an AR-4, a rig, a pack, a secure case and boots, fits an AR-4 magazine, packs three more magazines in the rig's first pouch and a splint in its third, and puts bandages, rations and water in the pack. The Inventory component's Starting Loadout gives a kit when play begins, on top of its Starting Items . The demo starts the player with its Starter kit. Kit up In the loadout library ( W_LoadoutLibrary ), the player picks a kit and presses Kit up with this . The screen raises Loadout.Apply , and the Inventory component kits the player up from what they own: Each worn piece is taken from the stash and put on. Whatever was worn in that slot goes back to the stash. Each fitted part is taken from the stash and fitted. Each packed item is moved from the stash into its bag or pouch until the count is met. Nothing is bought. A toast reads Kitted up with the kit's name, and says how many pieces were not in the stash. In Blueprint, Kit Up on the Inventory component does the same and returns that count. The loadout library. The chosen kit shows its worn pieces, its packed bags and its weight. Saved kits The stash screen's save button raises Loadout.Save , which adds Saved kit 1 , Saved kit 2 and so on to the library and opens it. Each one records what the player wears at that moment: th"}, {"t": "Layers and screens", "u": "layers-and-screens.html", "s": "Open and close screens from Blueprint, replace a shipped screen through the screen registry, how layers decide input and Escape, and how to write a screen of your own.", "h": ["Open a screen from Blueprint", "Replace a shipped screen", "The five layers", "Adding a layer", "Back and Escape", "Input", "Pushing from C++", "From inside a screen", "Writing your own screen", "Hooks a C++ subclass can override", "What you see"], "x": "Every Blackline screen lives on one of five layers, and every screen class names its own layer, so you never pick one when you open it. This page shows how to open and close screens from Blueprint, how to swap a shipped screen for your own, how layers decide input and Escape, and how to build a new screen. Open a screen from Blueprint The NVS View Screen Library puts screens on the viewport without any layer code. The first call that needs the layer stack builds it. Build the layers In your player controller's Begin Play, call Create Layer Stack with Self. With no layout given it uses the one your View Setup Definition names. Calling it again returns the stack already built. Show the toasts Call Show Screen By Tag with NVS.View.Screen.NotificationStack , so notifications have somewhere to draw. Open the hub Call Show Screen By Tag with NVS.View.Screen.MainMenu . The demo's player controller starts the game with exactly these three calls, after looking through its camera. Every screen behind the hub opens from the hub's own buttons. Call What it does Create Layer Stack Builds a container for every layer in a layout, binds them for the player and adds the root to the viewport. Show Screen By Tag Opens the screen the screen registry lists under an NVS.View.Screen.* tag, on the layer its class declares. Show Screen Opens a screen by class instead of by tag. Push To Layer Pushes any CommonUI activatable widget onto a layer you name. Close Screen By Tag Closes that screen wherever it is open. Close Top Screen Closes the topmost screen, the way Escape does. Clear Layer Closes every screen on one layer and returns how many closed. Is Screen Open True while that screen is open, on any layer. Open Loot Screen Opens the raid inventory with a crate's or a body's items beside the player's. The screen tags are declared by the plugin, one per shipped screen: MainMenu , InsertionSelect , RaidHUD , SkillsScreen , SkillTree , CharacterScreen , HealthScreen , StashScreen , LoadoutLibrary , RaidInventory , TraderList , TradeScreen , MarketScreen , TaskLog , PartyLobby , SettingsScreen , NotificationStack , RaidSummary , Hideout , BenchScreen and PowerStation , plus the smaller windows such as ItemMenu , InspectWindow and ContainerWindow . Each sits under NVS.View.Screen . See Screens . Replace a shipped screen The Screen Registry maps each screen tag to the Widget Blueprint that opens for it. The plugin ships DA_BlacklineScreenRegistry . Every screen that opens another goes through the registry too, so changing one row changes that screen everywhere. Make your screen Duplicate the shipped Widget Blueprint, such as /NVSBlackline/Screens/W_StashScreen , into your own folder, or make a child of it. Restyle or extend it in the designer. Make your registry Duplicate DA_BlacklineScreenRegistry into your folder and point the NVS.View.Screen.StashScreen row's Widget at your screen. Use it In your own View Setup Definition, set Screen Registry to your copy. A game's own setup wins over the one the plugin ships, so nothing else needs to change. Each registry row has a ScreenTag , a Widget and an optional LayerOverride . Only a screen can stand in for a screen, because the caller pushes it onto the layer that class declares. The five layers The layer table is a UNVSViewLayoutAsset . The plugin ships DA_BlacklineLayout , which the View Setup Definition names, with five layers from bottom to top: Tag Holds NVS.View.Layer.Game The raid HUD. NVS.View.Layer.GameMenu Screens over live gameplay: the raid inventory and the task log. NVS.View.Layer.Menu Full-screen menus, such as the hub and the stash. NVS.View.Layer.Modal The match found banner, the drop countdown, the debrief and the desk that container windows open on. NVS.View.Layer.Notification The notification stack and the item menu. Each row of the layout is an FNVSViewLayerDef : LayerTag A tag under NVS.View.Layer . Every push and pop addresses a layer by it. ZOrder Higher draws over lower. Declaration order does not matter. ContainerClass The CommonUI container. Leave it unset for a stack. InputConfig The FUIInputConfig applied when this layer is the highest occupied layer that decides input. bDecidesInput When false the layer draws but never takes input, so a toast layer can sit above a dialog without taking its input. bOwnsBackHandler Marks the layer meant to own Escape. The current Escape handling does not read it. TransitionDuration Zero cuts between screens, which is the default because a crossfade shows the world through both screens. In the editor, the asset's data validation reports a tag outside NVS.View.Layer or a tag used twice. Adding a layer Add a tag In Project Settings > Gameplay Tags , add a child of NVS.View.Layer , such as NVS.View.Layer.Tooltip . Copy the layout Duplicate DA_BlacklineLayout into your folder and add a row for the new tag with its z-order and input config. Keep the five shipped rows, because the shipped screens declare them. Use it Set Layout Asset on your View Setup Definition to your copy, or pass it to Create Layer Stack . Back and Escape The back keys close the topmost screen. They are Escape for the keyboard and the right face button for a gamepad, and the Engine Settings Setup Definition's Back Keys can name others. The layer subsystem reads them through a Slate input preprocessor, so they work even when no widget has focus. Layers are walked from the highest z-order down. The first active screen that is visible and does not ignore Back is chosen. The screen's InterceptBack runs first. The base class clears a selected tile or socket and returns true if it did, so the first press deselects and the second closes. The settings screen reverts a pending display change here. If nothing intercepted, the screen is removed from its layer and the one below it becomes active. Four class defaults on a screen change this: bIgnoresBack Back passes over this screen to the one below. The raid HUD and the notification stack set it, so Escape cl"}, {"t": "Viewmodels", "u": "viewmodels.html", "s": "How screens resolve context-keyed viewmodels from their sources, publish fields to widgets, decide when to show an empty state, and how to read a viewmodel from your own widgets.", "h": ["Where viewmodels sit", "Domain and context keys", "Where a screen's context comes from", "The viewmodel classes", "Empty states", "How widgets get the data", "Reading a viewmodel from your own widget", "Keeping them current", "What you see"], "x": "A viewmodel holds what one part of a screen shows. The screen asks a source for data, the viewmodel keeps it and publishes each field, and the widgets redraw when a field changes. This page explains how viewmodels are keyed, which ones exist, how a screen ties them to widgets, and when a screen shows its empty state. Where viewmodels sit An object that answers one of the nine source interfaces, such as a built-in source or your own Blueprint component. See Connecting your game . A UNVSViewModel subclass that reads the source through its interface and publishes the result as FieldNotify properties. The screen resolves one viewmodel per binding when it opens and releases them when it closes. Grids, rails and text blocks named in the screen's binding tables draw the published fields. Most games never touch a viewmodel directly. You change the source, and the viewmodel and widgets follow. Read on when you build your own screen or widget. Domain and context keys Every viewmodel is resolved under an FNVSViewModelKey : USTRUCT(BlueprintType) struct FNVSViewModelKey { /** What kind of viewmodel this is. */ FGameplayTag Domain; /** Which one. An unset Guid is legal and names the single instance of its domain. */ FGuid Context; }; The context is what lets a stash, a backpack and a looted crate each have their own container viewmodel at the same time. Unreal's MVVM collection keys on class and name alone, so UNVSViewModelRegistry , a local player subsystem, folds both halves into one name and counts holders on each key. UNVSViewModel* Resolve(const FNVSViewModelKey& Key, TSubclassOf<UNVSViewModel> ViewModelClass) Returns the instance for the key, creating it the first time. Pair every call with one Release . Null for an invalid key, a null class, or a key already resolved as another class. bool Release(const FNVSViewModelKey& Key) Drops one holder. True only when this released the last holder and destroyed the viewmodel. Screens call these for you. You only call the registry when you hold a viewmodel outside a screen. Where a screen's context comes from The shipped screens build each binding with UNVSViewScreen::MakeBinding , which derives the context from the binding id. A reopened screen therefore gets the same key, and two screens that name the same binding id share one viewmodel. The raid HUD, the raid inventory and the loadout library all bind Vitals , for example. When the player picks a record, such as a trader, the screen asks the binding again under that record's context, made from the record id. Your source receives these ids as its SetId , ContainerId or RecordId argument. The viewmodel classes All derive from UNVSViewModel , which derives from UMVVMViewModelBase . Domain tags sit under NVS.View.ViewModel . Class Domain tag Reads Has content when UNVSViewContainerViewModel Container INVSViewContainerSource It holds at least one item UNVSViewSlotSetViewModel SlotSet INVSViewSlotSetSource At least one slot is filled UNVSViewRecordViewModel Record INVSViewRecordSource It holds a record UNVSViewRecordListViewModel RecordList INVSViewRecordSource It holds at least one row UNVSViewMeterSetViewModel MeterSet INVSViewMeterSource It holds at least one meter UNVSViewFilterViewModel Filter INVSViewFilterSource Always UNVSViewMapMarkerListViewModel MapMarkerList INVSViewMapMarkerSource It holds at least one marker UNVSViewMarketViewModel Market INVSViewMarketSource It has listings or a catalogue UNVSViewSettingListViewModel SettingList INVSViewSettingValueSource Always UNVSViewNotifyQueueViewModel NotifyQueue The notification subsystem, through SetQueue Always Three more are rows that a list viewmodel fills, so they have no source of their own: UNVSViewMapMarkerViewModel , UNVSViewNotifyEntryViewModel and UNVSViewSettingRowViewModel . A record list fills UNVSViewRecordViewModel rows the same way. Each sourced viewmodel has the same entry points: bool SetSource(const TScriptInterface<Interface>& InSource, const FGuid& Context) Points the viewmodel at a source and a context, then reads it. The settings list takes a source only. bool BindSourceObject(UObject* SourceObject, const FGuid& ContextId) The same without naming the interface, which is what screens call. It checks ImplementsInterface , so Blueprint implementations work. False for an object that does not implement the interface. Blueprint callable. bool Refresh() Asks the current source again and publishes what changed. A selection held on the viewmodel survives. SetSource and Refresh are Blueprint callable on every class except the market viewmodel. Empty states A fresh install with no definition assets has nothing for most screens to list. Instead of drawing blank panels, fourteen screens show an empty state: a title, a sentence for the player, and a setup hint that names what to add. The decision comes from the viewmodels. UNVSViewModel::HasContent() returns false while the source has given the viewmodel nothing to show, as the table above lists. A screen names the bindings that matter in its EmptyWhenBindings array, and when every one of them comes up empty it lays its empty state over its content. The text comes from three more class defaults: EmptyTitle The title, such as \"No traders yet\". EmptyBody One or two sentences the player reads under the title. EmptySetupHint What a developer needs to add for the screen to fill, such as which data assets to create. Shown in the editor and development builds, never in a shipped game. Leave EmptyWhenBindings empty and a screen never shows one. The screens that do, and what each asks for, are listed on Screens . How widgets get the data The shipped screens author no Blueprint graph. A screen fills its widgets from tables on its class defaults, each naming widgets in its tree: ViewModelBindings One FNVSViewScreenBinding per viewmodel: BindingId , Domain , Context , ViewModelClass , and the source, as SourceObject or the built-in SourceClass . ContentBindings Which viewmodel fills which grid, socket or"}, {"t": "Theming", "u": "theming.html", "s": "Re-theme every screen from one style asset: colour roles, emphasis, type steps, fonts and layout tokens.", "h": ["The style asset", "Colour roles", "Emphasis", "Type steps", "Fonts", "Using your own font", "Layout tokens", "Reading tokens yourself", "Swap the style asset", "What you see"], "x": "Every colour, size, font weight and duration in Blackline comes from one data asset. Change the asset and every component and screen follows. This page covers what the asset holds and how to put your own in place. Try the main colour roles here. The preview uses the plugin's shipped values as its starting point. The style asset The theme is a UNVSViewStyleAsset . The plugin ships one, /NVSBlackline/DA_BlacklineStyle , and the plugin's View Setup Definition names it as its Style Asset . Components never hold a colour of their own. They ask the asset for a role, such as Accent or TextDim , and only the asset knows the value. Each component and each screen has a Style property. It is usually unset, and then the component uses the style asset of the View Setup Definition in force. If that names none that loads, it uses the class defaults of UNVSViewStyleAsset , which hold the same values as the shipped asset. The asset's maps: Colors TMap<ENVSViewColorRole, FLinearColor> . One entry per colour role. EmphasisAlphaScale TMap<ENVSViewEmphasis, float> . Alpha multipliers for states such as hover and disabled. TypeRamp TMap<ENVSViewTypeRole, FNVSViewTypeStep> . One text style per use. Spacing, Metrics, CornerRadii Gaps, fixed element sizes and corner radii, in pixels. DurationsSeconds, Easing, SlamEasing Animation lengths and cubic bezier curves, written the same way as in CSS. Font, WeightTypefaces The font asset text renders with, and which of its typefaces each weight uses. Colour roles ENVSViewColorRole has 121 roles. The ones you change first: Role Shipped value Used for Surface #0B0B0A Panel fill and the ground of most screens Accent #E7840E Selection, primary buttons, eyebrows TextPrimary #EDE7DB Body text and titles TextDim #8B8276 Secondary captions Success #3A9C5F Good news and positive figures Danger #E56A6A Bad news, such as an injury or a negative amount OnAccent #100E0B Text on a solid accent plate The rest cover specific states and surfaces: Border , SurfaceRaised and SurfaceInset , Critical and Warning , the button and toggle states ( AccentHover , AccentPressed , ToggleTrackOn ), and inks drawn over gameplay such as CrosshairDot and PromptInk . Each one carries a comment in Style/NVSViewStyleAsset.h saying where it is used. A role missing from Colors draws magenta, so a gap in a custom theme is easy to spot on screen. Emphasis Hover, pressed, disabled and similar states mostly reuse a role's colour with a different alpha. ENVSViewEmphasis names the steps and EmphasisAlphaScale holds the multiplier for each: Full 1.0, Raised 2.0, Reduced 0.55, Held 1.35, Faded 0.22, Lit 3.2, Struck 3.9. Tinted (0.13) and TintedRule (0.45) are absolute alphas for opaque category colours. Retune a step and every state that uses it changes together. Type steps Text styles are named by use, so the names still fit after you resize them. ENVSViewTypeRole has steps such as Label , Body , Caption , Heading , Control , Title , MenuEntry and a set of HUD steps. Each is a FNVSViewTypeStep : SizePx Size in pixels. Slate sizes fonts in points drawn at 96 dpi, so the components set three quarters of this value as the Slate size. Weight A CSS weight from 100 to 900. It picks a typeface through WeightTypefaces . LineHeight A multiplier on the size. LetterSpacingEm Tracking in em. For example, the shipped Title step is 26 px at weight 600, and Control , the caption inside a button, is 13 px at weight 600. Fonts The plugin ships JetBrains Mono in /NVSBlackline/Fonts/ : the composite font F_JetBrainsMono and its Regular, Medium, SemiBold and Bold faces. The shipped style asset uses it, with this weight map: 400 Regular 500 Medium 600 SemiBold 700 Bold Slate does no weight matching of its own, and an unknown typeface name silently falls back to the first one. So a weight with no exact entry uses the nearest mapped weight, preferring the heavier one on a tie. JetBrains Mono is under the SIL Open Font License 1.1. The licence text ships in the plugin at Licenses/JetBrainsMono-OFL.txt , and CREDITS.md lists it. You can ship it in your game under that licence. Using your own font Import the font Import your font files and create a composite Font asset with one typeface entry per weight. Point the asset at it In your style asset, set Font to the new Font asset. Map the weights Edit WeightTypefaces so each weight the type ramp uses names a typeface entry in your font. FontFamily and FontFallbacks are for reference only. Slate cannot load an operating system font by name, so Font is what renders. With Font unset, text uses Slate's default font. Layout tokens Spacing (ENVSViewSpacingStep) Micro 3, Tiny 4, Small 6, Base 8, Medium 10, Large 12, Section 16, Region 32. Region is the gap between a label column and its control. Metrics (ENVSViewMetricRole) Fixed sizes such as GridCell (60, the painted inventory cell), ButtonHeight , SlotTile , DollCell and BeltCell . CornerRadii (ENVSViewCornerRadius) Sharp 0, Soft 3 for outlined boxes, Pill 1000. DurationsSeconds (ENVSViewDurationStep) Fast 0.08, Standard 0.12, Slow 0.16, Entrance 0.20, plus the deploy sequence steps such as Slam 0.42. Reading tokens yourself Your own widgets can read the same tokens. The asset's getters are Blueprint pure: const UNVSViewStyleAsset* Style = UNVSViewSettings::GetStyleAsset(); if (Style != nullptr) { const FLinearColor Accent = Style->GetColor(ENVSViewColorRole::Accent); const FLinearColor Hovered = Style->GetColorAtEmphasis(ENVSViewColorRole::Border, ENVSViewEmphasis::Raised); const FNVSViewTypeStep Body = Style->GetTypeStep(ENVSViewTypeRole::Body); const float Gap = Style->GetSpacing(ENVSViewSpacingStep::Base); } A widget derived from UNVSViewStyledWidget also has ResolveColor , ResolveTypeStep , ResolveSpacing and the rest, which respect its own Style override. Swap the style asset Duplicate the shipped asset Copy DA_BlacklineStyle into your own content folder, so a plugin update never overwrites your theme. Edit it Change the roles in Colors , "}, {"t": "Settings", "u": "settings.html", "s": "The settings screen: the registry that describes each row, the source that stores values, the Engine Settings Setup Definition, how display changes are kept or reverted, key bindings, and adding a row of your own.", "h": ["What works out of the box", "The Engine Settings Setup Definition", "Describe, then store", "Keeping or reverting a change", "Driving the clock yourself", "Key bindings", "Adding a setting row", "1. Describe it", "2. Store it", "3. Point the screen at them", "4. Read it in gameplay", "Things to know", "What you see"], "x": "The settings screen draws its rows from a registry that describes each setting, and reads and writes values through a source your game controls. The shipped screen applies every row to the engine straight away, so window mode, volumes, language and the rest work with no code. This page covers both halves, how a display change is kept or reverted, and how to add a row of your own. Settings, with the category rail, the chosen category's rows and the Apply bar. What works out of the box The shipped W_SettingsScreen uses three classes from the NVSViewSettings module: Class Role UNVSViewEngineSettingRegistry The rows of a typical shooter's settings screen, in six categories: Settings.Video , Settings.Audio , Settings.Controls , Settings.Gameplay , Settings.Interface and Settings.Accessibility . The Controls category also gets one key binding row per bindable action. UNVSViewEngineSettingCategories The category rail, with each category's name and description. UNVSViewEngineSettingsSource Reads and writes each row wherever the engine keeps it: the game user settings, console variables, the sound mix and the current culture. Values the engine has no home for are saved in UNVSViewSavedSettings , in GameUserSettings.ini . Saved values are applied again once a player has a controller, by UNVSViewPlayerSettingsSubsystem . Rows the engine cannot act on, such as the Gameplay rows, subtitle size and the high contrast HUD, are saved for your game to read. The accessibility ones say Read by the game on the screen. #include \"Settings/NVSViewSavedSettings.h\" const UNVSViewSavedSettings& Saved = UNVSViewSavedSettings::Get(); const bool bAutoReload = Saved.IsAutoReload(); const int32 SubtitleSize = Saved.GetSubtitleSize(); // 0 small, 1 medium, 2 large const float Fov = Saved.GetFieldOfView(); Each setting id names its tab and its setting, such as Video.WindowMode , Audio.MasterVolume or Gameplay.AutoReload . The full list is in Settings/NVSViewSettingIds.h . The Engine Settings Setup Definition How the shipped rows reach the engine is set on one data asset, the Engine Settings Setup Definition . The plugin ships DA_BlacklineEngineSetup . Make your own from right-click > Miscellaneous > Data Asset , and a game's own setup wins over the one the plugin ships. Sound Mix, Master Class, Music Class, Effects Class, Voice Class, Interface Class The sound mix and sound classes the five volume sliders drive. Point them at your own to make the sliders control your game's audio. Night Mode Effect Added to the master submix while dynamic range is set to Night. Drive Field Of View, Authored Field Of View Scales each player's camera field of view by the saved value against the one your cameras are built with. Turn it off if your game sets field of view itself. Revert Window Seconds How long a display change waits for the player to keep it. Default 15. Max Visible Notifications, Max Held Notifications The notification stack's sizes. See Notifications . Back Keys Keys that close the screen on top. Escape and the gamepad's right face button by default. Action Set The actions the Controls tab lists for rebinding. Context Priority The priority of the mapping context built from the player's bindings. Keep it above your game's own contexts, so a rebound key wins. Describe, then store Blackline never keeps a setting's value itself. A UNVSViewSettingRegistry describes each row, and the value always travels through INVSViewSettingValueSource , so your save system can stay the only store. A registry is a data asset, so you can also make one and fill its rows by hand. Each row is an FNVSViewSettingDescription : SettingId, CategoryId The row's id, and the id of the category it is listed under. Kind Bool , Enum , Slider or Keybind . It picks the row widget and which field of the value it reads. ApplyModel Live or ConfirmAndRevert . See below. Label, HelpText The row's caption and the line under it. Options, OptionValues, Presentation A choice row's choices, what each stands for (such as 60 for a 60 FPS cap), and how it draws them. Auto uses chips for up to four choices and a stepper past that. DefaultValue What a player starts with on first launch: a slider's number, 1 for a switch that starts on, or a choice's place in Options counting from 0. MinValue, MaxValue, StepSize, ValueSuffix, DisplayFractionalDigits A slider's range and readout. A step of zero is continuous. KeyMappingName For a key binding row, the action it binds. A value is an FNVSViewSettingValue . Kind says which field is used: bBoolValue , OptionIndex or Number . Key binding rows carry no value field, because the player's key profile stores bindings. Keeping or reverting a change Most settings are Live and take effect when written. Settings that can leave the screen unreadable, such as window mode and resolution, are ConfirmAndRevert . They apply provisionally and go back unless the player keeps them. The clock lives in UNVSViewSettingApplySubsystem , a local player subsystem, so closing the screen cannot skip a revert. The window opens on the first provisional change, later changes do not extend it, and it counts down while the game is paused. A revert is an ordinary write of the old value through the source. The player changes rows. Each change is held, the row shows the new value and the bar counts the unsaved changes. Nothing is written yet. The player presses Apply. Every held change goes to the apply subsystem, and key binding drafts are saved. A Live row is written through SetSettingValue straight away. A ConfirmAndRevert row first reads and keeps its old value, then writes the new one. A veil asks \"Keep these settings?\" with a countdown. Keep, or Enter, confirms. Revert, Escape, or the countdown reaching zero writes the old values back. A display change waits for the player to keep it. Closing the screen without Apply throws held changes away. Untick bChangesWaitForApply on a copy of the screen to write each change as it is made. A Video change is saved only on"}, {"t": "Notifications", "u": "notifications.html", "s": "Tell the player something with a toast: Notification Kind Definition assets, pushing from Blueprint or C++, priority, repeats, holding toasts back during menus, and actionable toasts.", "h": ["Push a toast from Blueprint", "Notification Kind Definition", "The built-in kinds", "The payload", "The queue", "Capacity", "Repeats", "Holding back during menus", "Removing toasts", "Toasts the player acts on", "Showing the stack", "Push from C++", "What you see"], "x": "Gameplay tells the player something by pushing a notification to the queue. The queue merges repeats, ranks by priority, holds less urgent toasts back while a menu is open, and the notification stack draws what is left. You describe each kind of toast as a data asset and push it with one Blueprint call. The notification stack, bottom right, with toasts of several kinds. Push a toast from Blueprint The quickest way is a Notification Kind Definition asset, which holds everything about a kind of toast except its words. Make the kind Create a data asset from right-click > Miscellaneous > Data Asset and pick Notification Kind Definition . Give it a tag, such as MyGame.Notify.Extract , a priority, a duration and its colours. The plugin registers the type with the Asset Manager, so it is found without configuration. Get the queue In your player controller, add Get NVSNotifySubsystem , the local player subsystem getter, with Self as the player controller. Push it Call Push Kind on the subsystem with your kind asset, a title and a body. It returns false when the queue refused it. Without a kind asset, call Push with a Make NVSNotifyPayload node instead and set the tag, priority and duration yourself. The demo does this when the player marks an item as junk. It pushes a payload tagged NVS.View.Notify.System at priority 1 for three seconds. Notification Kind Definition A toast takes the look of the kind whose tag matches its own most closely, so one kind can style a whole family of tags. Tag The notifications this kind styles. A child tag, such as MyGame.Notify.Extract.Late , takes this kind unless a closer kind exists. Priority From 0, the most urgent, to 4, the least. An urgent toast pushes a less urgent one out of a full stack. Duration Seconds on screen. Zero or less stays until dismissed. Action Tag When set, the toast shows an action button and a dismiss button. See Toasts the player acts on . Accent, Title Role, Body Role, Surface, Border Colour roles from the theme for each part of the toast. See Theming . Draws Edge Draws the accent as a bar down the toast's left edge. Title Is Status Puts the title at the right of the line as a status word instead of above the body. A kind asset comes before the plugin's own look for a tag, so you can also restyle a built-in kind by making an asset with its tag. The built-in kinds Without any kind assets, the toast still knows these tags, which the plugin declares under NVS.View.Notify : Tag Look System Danger caption on the system plate, with the title as a status word. Status Critical edge on a red plate, with the title as a status word. CrewDown Critical edge and caption on a red plate. Exfil , Market.Success Success edge and caption on a green plate. Task Accent edge and caption on an accent plate. Market.Warning Warning edge and caption on an overlay card. Market.Danger Danger edge and caption on a red plate. Progression , TraderOffer , Market.Info Plain overlay card with a dim caption. Salvage Plain overlay card with a muted caption. A tag that matches no kind draws as an overlay card with an accent edge and caption. The payload A notification is an FNVSNotifyPayload . Push Kind fills the tag, priority, duration and action tag from the kind, and you give the words. Tag The identity, matched exactly. A second push with the same tag updates the first toast instead of adding one. A payload with no tag is refused. Priority Smaller is more urgent. Defaults to 4, the least urgent, so a payload that sets none never pushes out one that does. Title, Body The toast's two lines. Duration Seconds on screen. Defaults to 4. Zero or less stays until dismissed. ActionTag Makes the toast one the player can act on. Leave it empty for a toast that only informs. Notifications are local to one player's machine. Push them where the replicated state arrives, such as a replication callback or a client event. The queue UNVSNotifySubsystem is a local player subsystem and ticks on the core ticker, so toasts count down while the game is paused. Everything pushed goes into one queue, one entry per tag, in arrival order. Three rules decide what shows. Capacity Up to four toasts show at once, and an open menu holds back up to four more. The Engine Settings Setup Definition's Max Visible Notifications and Max Held Notifications change both. The player's Notification density setting lowers what shows to one or three, or shows the full number on All. When the stack is full, an arrival pushes out the least urgent entry, the oldest among equals, but only if the arrival is strictly more urgent. Otherwise the push returns false and the arrival is dropped. Repeats A push whose tag is already queued replaces that entry's payload, priority included, adds one to its repeat count and restarts its timer. The toast keeps its place in the stack. Use one tag for a running ticker, such as salvage picked up, and it updates in place. Holding back during menus While the Menu layer has a screen, entries at priority 2 and below are held back. Priority 0 and 1 always show. Held entries do not count down. When the menu closes they rejoin the stack, least urgent dropped first if more come back than the stack holds, and the stack writes how many it is holding under the toasts. Your code pushes a task toast at priority 2 while the player has the stash open. No entry has that tag, so a new entry is made with its timer. The Menu layer is occupied and priority 2 can wait, so the entry joins the held group. Its timer does not run. The player closes the stash. The queue releases the held entries. The entry joins the stack and its timer starts. The stack redraws. When its duration runs out the entry is removed and the stack redraws again. Removing toasts Dismiss removes the entry under one tag at once, persistent or not, and returns false when nothing was queued under it. Dismiss All empties the queue. Both are Blueprint callable, as are the read functions Get Visible Entries , Get Suppressed Count , Is Menu Ope"}, {"t": "Rendering", "u": "rendering.html", "s": "How Blackline renders item icons and character figures into the UI, which screens animate their figure, the preview profiles, the render settings on the View Setup Definition, and what each costs.", "h": ["Where the pictures come from", "Which screens move their figure", "The parts", "Icons", "Sizes and sharing", "Budget and eviction", "Live previews", "Preview profiles", "Framing", "Render settings", "The cutout material", "What it costs", "Setting up your own meshes", "What you see"], "x": "Blackline draws real 3D meshes in the UI in two ways. Item tiles get icons rendered once and cached, and the character, health, inspect, lobby and paper doll views get a live figure. This page explains both, which screens move their figure, what it costs, and how to set up your own meshes. Item tiles draw icons rendered from each item's look. The character screen is the one place the figure idles and turns. Where the pictures come from Each item gets its picture from its Item Definition: Icon A picture to use instead of a rendered icon. Leave it empty to render one from the look. Look A Static Mesh , a Skeletal Mesh or a Pickup Actor such as a fully built rifle, and its framing on tiles and in the inspect window. Worn On, Worn Offset On the wearable types, Weapon, Armour, Gear and Container. Where the Look mesh hangs on the character while worn. Left as None it shows nothing, except a Gear, Armour or Container item with only a Skeletal Mesh , which follows the body like clothing. So a rifle that takes two cells and a magazine that takes one are drawn from their own models at whatever size each tile needs, and parts fitted to a gun show on its picture everywhere. The figure is dressed from your Wardrobe Piece Definitions, and the gear the player's inventory wears replaces the figure's own equipment. With no wardrobe pieces, the built-in look source shows a mannequin of basic shapes. Anything not described by a definition is asked of the look source. See the look source and Character looks . Which screens move their figure Every placement of the figure uses a preview profile . Only the character screen animates its figure or lets the player turn it. No preview zooms in on a body part or swoops between views, because that motion made players dizzy. The plugin ships four profiles in /NVSBlackline/Render/ : Profile Used by Pose Turn by dragging Zoom Redraw PP_Character Character screen Animated idle Yes No Live PP_Doll Paper doll Still No No Live PP_Portrait Lobby crew cards Still No No On change PP_Inspect Inspect window, which shows an item Still Yes Yes, up to 2 On change All four frame the full body and none uses focus. The inspect window turns and zooms the item it shows, never the figure. The parts Everything here lives in the NVSViewRender module. FNVSViewStudio An isolated preview world with a light rig. It is not a level and nothing in it plays. Every icon and figure is built here, far from your game world. UNVSViewIconRenderer A game instance subsystem that owns the studio, the icon cache and the look source. It renders a few icons per frame and ticks the live figures. UNVSViewCharacterPreview A UMG widget that shows a live capture of a figure or an item. UNVSViewPreviewProfile A data asset that sets how one placement of the preview behaves. FNVSViewLookAssembly The components one look is built from in the studio. The icon renderer and the preview both use it. M_IconCutout The UI material that turns a capture into a cut-out with a transparent background. The renderer is not created on a dedicated server, so a server pays nothing for any of this. Icons A tile asks the renderer for its item's icon. The renderer reads the item's look, builds it in a studio slot, captures it with an orthographic camera into a render target and keeps the target in a cache. The tile draws the texture through the cutout material once it is ready. Slots sit 100,000 units apart, so no capture can see a neighbour. Sizes and sharing Icon sizes double from 64 pixels along the long side up to the Icon Max Pixels cap, 512 by default, so tiles of similar size share one render. The short side follows the item's footprint and never drops below 16 pixels. The size a tile asks for is multiplied by the viewport's UI scale, and when the scale changes, icons still on screen are rendered again at the new size. The cache key covers the size and everything visible in the look: meshes, materials, offsets, tints, scalars and the framing override. Two items with the same look share one render, and a change to any of those gives a new icon. Budget and eviction At most Icons Per Frame icons render each frame. The rest wait in a queue, and their assets load asynchronously first. In the editor, a look whose shaders or textures are still compiling would capture grey. The renderer waits and retries every quarter second, for up to 30 seconds. A cooked game has every shader ready and skips the wait. Each cached icon costs 8 bytes a pixel. A 512 by 512 icon is 2 MB. When the cache goes over Icon Cache Megabytes , icons no widget holds are evicted, oldest use first. Icons on screen are never evicted, and if every icon is held the log warns once a minute. Live previews UNVSViewCharacterPreview is a widget you can place in a Widget Blueprint. Profile The preview profile for this placement. Empty uses the class defaults: an animated idle, full body framing and drag to turn. ContextId Whose figure to show. Empty means the local player. ItemId An item to show instead of the figure, seen from its icon angle. None shows the figure. bItemsOnly Shows items only. With no item set it shows nothing instead of the figure. RenderSize The capture size in pixels, 512 by 768 by default. Match the slot's shape so the subject is not letterboxed small. bFillsSlot Fills the slot edge to edge and captures at its size. RenderSize then only sets the first frame. While a look is still loading the preview draws loading dots. When the look changes, the preview compares the new look with the one it built. If the base is the same and every part has a unique name, it swaps only the parts that changed, so changing a helmet does not reload the body. Preview profiles A profile is a UNVSViewPreviewProfile data asset. Make one from right-click > Miscellaneous > Data Asset and set it on a preview's Profile . Pose AnimatedIdle loops the look's Character Preview Animation . Static holds its first frame. Framing, FramingZone FullBody fits the whole figure, UpperBody moves in"}, {"t": "Screens", "u": "screens.html", "s": "All twenty-one screens with their codes, assets, layers and tags, what fills each one, and how empty states work.", "h": ["All screens", "How a screen is filled", "Empty states", "Set or change an empty state", "01 Hub", "What fills it", "Bindings", "02 Location select", "What fills it", "Bindings", "03 Raid HUD", "What fills it", "Bindings", "04 Skills", "What fills it", "Bindings", "04B Skill tree", "What fills it", "Bindings", "04C Character", "What fills it", "Bindings", "04D Health", "What fills it", "Bindings", "05 Stash", "What fills it", "Bindings", "06 Loadouts", "What fills it", "Bindings", "07 Raid inventory", "What fills it", "Bindings", "08 Traders", "What fills it", "Bindings", "09 Trade", "What fills it", "Bindings", "09B Market", "What fills it", "Bindings", "10 Task log", "What fills it", "Bindings", "11 Lobby", "What fills it", "Bindings", "12 Settings", "What fills it", "Bindings", "13 Notifications", "What fills it", "Bindings", "14 Debrief", "What fills it", "Bindings", "15 Hideout", "What fills it", "Bindings", "15B Workbench", "What fills it", "Bindings", "15C Generator", "What fills it", "Bindings", "Windows and overlays"], "x": "Blackline ships 21 screens as Widget Blueprints in /NVSBlackline/Screens . This page lists each one with what it is for, what fills it, and the empty state it shows before your project has that content. All screens The code is the number this site and the Blackline Demo use for each screen. Open any of them from Blueprint with Show Screen By Tag and the tag in the last column. Code Screen Asset Layer Screen tag Empty state 01 Hub W_MainMenu Menu NVS.View.Screen.MainMenu No 02 Location select W_InsertionSelect Menu NVS.View.Screen.InsertionSelect Yes 03 Raid HUD W_RaidHUD Game NVS.View.Screen.RaidHUD No 04 Skills W_SkillsScreen Menu NVS.View.Screen.SkillsScreen Yes 04B Skill tree W_SkillTree Menu NVS.View.Screen.SkillTree Yes 04C Character W_CharacterScreen Menu NVS.View.Screen.CharacterScreen Yes 04D Health W_HealthScreen Menu NVS.View.Screen.HealthScreen No 05 Stash W_StashScreen Menu NVS.View.Screen.StashScreen Yes 06 Loadouts W_LoadoutLibrary Menu NVS.View.Screen.LoadoutLibrary Yes 07 Raid inventory W_RaidInventory GameMenu NVS.View.Screen.RaidInventory No 08 Traders W_TraderList Menu NVS.View.Screen.TraderList Yes 09 Trade W_TradeScreen Menu NVS.View.Screen.TradeScreen Yes 09B Market W_MarketScreen Menu NVS.View.Screen.MarketScreen Yes 10 Task log W_TaskLog GameMenu NVS.View.Screen.TaskLog Yes 11 Lobby W_PartyLobby Menu NVS.View.Screen.PartyLobby No 12 Settings W_SettingsScreen Menu NVS.View.Screen.SettingsScreen No 13 Notifications W_NotificationStack Notification NVS.View.Screen.NotificationStack No 14 Debrief W_RaidSummary Modal NVS.View.Screen.RaidSummary Yes 15 Hideout W_Hideout Menu NVS.View.Screen.Hideout Yes 15B Workbench W_BenchScreen Menu NVS.View.Screen.BenchScreen Yes 15C Generator W_PowerStation Menu NVS.View.Screen.PowerStation Yes Menu screens share a bottom bar that moves between Hub, Stash, Hideout, Traders, Market and Lobby. Every screen closes with Escape except the raid HUD and the notifications, which Escape passes over. How a screen is filled Each screen has a list of bindings in its class defaults, ViewModelBindings . Every binding has an id, a viewmodel class and a built-in source class. The built-in sources read your data assets, the player's Inventory component and the plugin's own subsystems, so most screens fill as soon as the matching data assets exist. Screens without a matching kind of data asset, such as the health zones, the lobby crew or the HUD compass, read sources whose rows you fill in yourself. You can do that with no code at all. Create a data asset whose class is the built-in source, such as NVSViewBodyZones , fill in its rows, then create a Screen Source Definition with Replaces set to that class and Asset set to your data asset. The demo does this for every such screen, with pairs such as Demo/Data/DA_PlayerBody and Demo/Sources/PDA_PlayerBodySource . To feed a binding from live game state instead, make a Blueprint component that implements the binding's interface, pass it to Set Screen Source naming the built-in source it replaces, and call Refresh Source when its values change. The full order a screen picks its sources in is on Architecture , and the interfaces a source can answer are on Interfaces . Buttons that change game data raise a request on the request subsystem. See Requests . Empty states A fresh install has no content, so a screen that lists content would open blank. Instead, fourteen screens show an empty state card in the middle of the screen. It has three lines: A title, such as No traders yet . One or two sentences for the player. A setup hint for you, in the accent colour, naming what to create. It ends by saying the demo project shows the screen filled. The hint appears in the editor and in Development builds. A Shipping build never draws it. The card shows when every binding the screen lists in EmptyWhenBindings comes up empty, such as a list with no rows or a set of worn slots with nothing in them. It goes away by itself as soon as one of them has content. The screen is still there underneath and clicks pass through the card, so anything the screen can still do keeps working. Screen Shows when these are empty Title For the player Setup hint 02 Location select Markers No locations There is nowhere to deploy to yet. Create Raid Map Definition data assets, each with its level, exits and a point on the region map. 04 Skills Skills No skills yet Skills you raise by playing will appear here. Create Skill Definition and Branch Definition data assets. 04B Skill tree PerkTree No perks yet Perks you can take will appear here. Create Perk Definition and Branch Definition data assets. 04C Character Wardrobe Nothing to wear yet Outfits and gear for your character will appear here. Create Wardrobe Piece Definition data assets, including a body piece. 05 Stash Figure , Stash Nothing in your stash What you own and wear will appear here. Add an Inventory component to the player's pawn and give it starting items. 06 Loadouts Library No saved kits Kits you save will appear here. Create Loadout Definition data assets, or let players save their own from the stash. 08 Traders Roster No traders yet Traders you can deal with will appear here. Create Trader Definition data assets. 09 Trade Vendor , Stock No trader to deal with This trader has nothing on the shelf yet. Create Trader Definition data assets and list what each one sells. 09B Market Market The market is empty Nobody is selling anything right now. The market lists Item Definition data assets that have a price, or your own market source. 10 Task log Tasks No tasks Tasks from traders will appear here once you take them. Create Task Definition data assets and give them to a trader. 14 Debrief RaidReport No raid to report Your last raid's result will appear here. Call End Raid on the raid session when a raid finishes. 15 Hideout Stations No stations built Your hideout's stations will appear here. Create Station Definition data assets, such as a workbench or a generator. 15B Workbench Benc"}, {"t": "Components", "u": "components.html", "s": "The reusable widget components in NVSViewWidgets, grouped by job, with their main editable properties and events.", "h": ["How components work", "Foundations", "UNVSViewStyledWidget", "Text, W_Text", "Button, W_Button", "Text input, W_TextInput", "Key hint, W_KeyHint", "UNVSViewCutBorder", "Navigation", "Tab header, W_TabHeader", "Sub nav, W_SubNav", "Bottom chrome, W_BottomChrome", "Menu row, W_MenuRow", "Items and containers", "Grid view, W_InventoryGrid", "Item tile, W_ItemTile", "Equip slot, W_EquipSlot", "Belt cell, W_BeltCell", "Paper doll, W_PaperDoll", "Container block, W_ContainerBlock", "Search veil, W_SearchVeil, and search badge, W_SearchBadge", "Drop slot, W_DropSlot", "FNVSViewItemSubject", "Record rows and cards", "UNVSViewListEntry", "Record row, W_LoadoutRow", "Record card, W_RecordCard, and perk card, W_PerkCard", "Figure row, W_FigureRow, and meter row, W_MeterRow", "HUD row, W_ExfilRow and W_CrewRow", "Objective row, W_ObjectiveRow", "Chat line, W_ChatLine", "Standing, W_Standing", "Crew card, W_CrewCard", "Meters and readouts", "Stat meter, W_StatMeter, W_LimbBar and W_LoadBar", "Encumbrance, W_Encumbrance", "Signature, W_Signature", "Body zone, W_BodyZone", "Generator, W_Generator", "Cards and tiles", "UNVSViewPlateTile", "Catalogue tile, W_CatalogueTile", "Station card, W_StationCard", "Trader card, W_TraderCard", "Upgrade row, W_UpgradeRow", "Market", "Listing row, W_ListingRow", "Catalogue row, W_CatalogueRow", "Bid row, W_BidRow", "Settings rows", "UNVSViewSettingRow", "Bool row, W_SettingRowBool", "Choice row, W_SettingRowEnum", "Slider row, W_SettingRowSlider", "Key-bind row, W_SettingRowKeybind", "UNVSViewSettingDropdown", "Input test strip, W_InputTestStrip", "Notifications", "Toast, W_Toast"], "x": "The screens are built from about fifty themed components in the NVSViewWidgets module. You can place them in your own Widget Blueprints. This page says what each one is for, which properties you set, and which events you can bind. How components work Every component except the button, tab strips and cut border derives from UNVSViewStyledWidget . Components never hold a colour, size or duration of their own. They name a role, such as ENVSViewColorRole::Accent or ENVSViewTypeRole::Heading , and resolve it from the style asset. Leave the Style property unset to use the style asset of the View Setup Definition in force. See Theming . Most C++ classes are Abstract and have a shipped Widget Blueprint in /NVSBlackline/Widgets , named in the headings below, which carries the widget tree. Base classes have none. Subclass that asset, or place it as it is. Most components also expose many colour role properties, one per state. They are left out below because they only retint. Events come in two kinds. Properties marked Blueprint below are BlueprintAssignable and appear in a Blueprint's event list. The rest are native delegates you bind from C++. // A native event, bound from C++. Tile->OnChosen().AddUObject(this, &UMyWidget::HandleTileChosen); // A Blueprint event, also bindable from C++ as a dynamic delegate. Tile->OnDoubleClicked.AddDynamic(this, &UMyWidget::HandleDoubleClicked); Foundations UNVSViewStyledWidget The base for most components. It holds the optional Style asset and the resolvers, all Blueprint pure: ResolveColor , ResolveColorAtEmphasis , ResolveSpacing , ResolveMetric , ResolveTypeStep and ResolveDurationSeconds . Derive your own components from it to share the theme. Text, W_Text UNVSViewText is one line of text in a colour role and a type step. Headings, eyebrows and notes differ only in those two. Text , ColorRole , TypeRole . What it says and how it looks. bWrap . Wraps onto further lines. Off by default. LineHeight . Overrides the step's line height. Zero keeps the step's own. Justification . Horizontal alignment. Blueprint callable: SetText , SetColorRole , SetTypeRole . Button, W_Button UNVSViewButton derives from UCommonButtonBase , so it keeps CommonUI's focus, hold and input action behaviour. It maps the reported state (normal, hovered, pressed, selected, disabled, focused) to token colours. Label . The caption. A button bound to an input action shows the action's name instead. Variant . A preset of roles, one of Secondary , Primary , GhostChip , Positive , Danger , Clipped , RailChip , MenuRow or Tab . Changing it rewrites the role properties, so set the variant before tuning roles by hand. SubText , SelectedSubText . A second line under the caption. CountText . A figure in the plate's corner, such as a filter count. KeyCapText . A key drawn in a ruled square before the caption, such as ESC. Icon , bShowIcon , bIconTrailing , bShowIconPlate . An icon and where it sits. FillFraction , FillRole . A progress fill across the plate. PlateRadius , PlateCut , PlatePadding , PlateSize . The plate's shape and size. StyleTokens . The style asset. Named this way because the base class already has a Style property. Blueprint callable: SetLabel . Click events are CommonUI's own, such as OnClicked() . Text input, W_TextInput UNVSViewTextInput is a single-line field for search and chat. It wraps UEditableTextBox and applies every colour from the theme. Hint . Shown while the field is empty. Variant . Either Search (a plate with an icon, a count and a clear button) or InlineRename (a bottom rule only, at a larger size). CountText . A figure at the right, such as 42 ITEMS. bShowsClearMark , LeadingIcon , ClearIcon . Blueprint callable: GetText , SetText , SetHint . Native events: OnCommitted() on Enter and OnEdited() on every change, both with the text. Key hint, W_KeyHint UNVSViewKeyHint is an input action icon with a caption. The icon, name and hold state come from the wrapped UCommonActionWidget . Label , KeyText (the letter drawn while no action is bound), Form ( Caption , Prompt or Quiet ). Blueprint callable: SetEnhancedInputAction , SetInputActionRow , SetInputActionBinding , SetLabel . UNVSViewCutBorder A UBorder with two opposite corners cut off, drawn as a polygon. CornerCut sets the cut in local units. Zero paints an ordinary border. It has no Widget Blueprint and appears in the palette like any border. Navigation Tab header, W_TabHeader UNVSViewTabHeader is a themed UCommonTabListWidgetBase . CommonUI keeps the tab registry, selection and button pool. It adds active and inactive label and rule colours and passes its StyleTokens to each tab button. Blueprint pure: IsTabActive(FName TabNameID) . Sub nav, W_SubNav UNVSViewSubNav is the contextual tab row at the top of a sectioned screen. It grows taller only when a tab carries a portrait plate, and draws nothing when the screen registers no tabs. FaceStandIn sets the text in an empty portrait. Screens fill it from their TabBindings . Bottom chrome, W_BottomChrome UNVSViewBottomChrome is the bottom bar with section tabs, contextual buttons and Deploy. Deploy follows the deploy queue's state wherever the bar is drawn. DeployCaption . Deploy's resting caption. LeaveCaption . Empty draws no leave button. SectionWithOwnDeploy . The section whose screen has its own deploy button. Default Crew . DeployCut , LockedOpacity . A screen describes its contextual buttons with FNVSViewChromeAction ( ButtonName , Caption , Variant , bQueueHero ) in its ChromeActions array, and binds them by name. Menu row, W_MenuRow UNVSViewMenuRow is one item menu row with its word on the left and its key on the right. Label , KeyText , bActs (a row that cannot act is dimmed and takes no click) and InertOpacity . Items and containers See Items , Containers and Drag and drop for how screens use these. Grid view, W_InventoryGrid UNVSViewGridView draws a cell lattice and places one tile per entry. It never touches data. A drop fires an event and the screen raises"}, {"t": "Source interfaces", "u": "source-interfaces.html", "s": "The nine interfaces Blackline's screens read, with every function, struct field and the order a screen picks its source in.", "h": ["How a screen gets its data", "Which source a binding uses", "The nine interfaces", "The context id", "When a screen reads again", "INVSViewContainerSource", "INVSViewFilterSource", "INVSViewLookSource", "INVSViewMapMarkerSource", "INVSViewMarketSource", "INVSViewMeterSource", "A meter source in C++", "INVSViewRecordSource", "INVSViewSettingValueSource", "INVSViewSlotSetSource", "Next steps"], "x": "This is the full list behind Connecting your game : the nine interfaces the screens read, the order a screen picks its source in, and every field of the structs they return. Read the guide first. You only need this page for a value the guide does not cover, or to write a source in C++. How a screen gets its data A screen never reads your game's types. Each of its bindings asks a source for plain structs, such as a list of meters or a container's items, and a viewmodel publishes them to the widgets. Every binding names a built-in source class, such as UNVSViewVitals for the raid HUD's vitals, and your object stands in for that class. Which source a binding uses A screen binding resolves its source in this order. The first one found wins: An object passed to SetBindingSource on that open screen. The binding's SourceObject , set on a screen's class defaults. An object registered with Set Screen Source for the binding's built-in class. A Screen Source Definition that replaces the binding's built-in class. A new instance of the binding's built-in SourceClass . SetBindingSource is Blueprint callable on any screen. Use it to point one binding of one open screen at a live object. SetInventoryOwner does the same for every container binding at once. The nine interfaces All nine live in the NVSView module under Public/Interfaces . Each is Blueprintable and every function is a BlueprintNativeEvent , so a Blueprint can implement it from Class Settings. The default implementation returns nothing, and a binding whose source returns nothing shows nothing. Interface Read by Built-in sources INVSViewContainerSource UNVSViewContainerViewModel UNVSViewItemGrid and its subclasses, UNVSViewLootBody , UNVSViewCrewGear , UNVSViewInventorySource INVSViewFilterSource UNVSViewFilterViewModel UNVSViewFilterOptions , UNVSViewLootTabs , UNVSViewWardrobeFilter , UNVSViewInventorySource INVSViewLookSource The icon renderer and the character preview UNVSViewCharacterLooks INVSViewMapMarkerSource UNVSViewMapMarkerListViewModel UNVSViewMapMarkers INVSViewMarketSource UNVSViewMarketViewModel UNVSViewMarketBoard INVSViewMeterSource UNVSViewMeterSetViewModel UNVSViewMeterList and its subclasses, plus the loadout, body zone, trader job, player profile and crew gear sources INVSViewRecordSource UNVSViewRecordViewModel , UNVSViewRecordListViewModel UNVSViewRecordList and its many subclasses INVSViewSettingValueSource UNVSViewSettingListViewModel , UNVSViewSettingApplySubsystem UNVSViewSettingValues , and UNVSViewEngineSettingsSource in NVSViewSettings INVSViewSlotSetSource UNVSViewSlotSetViewModel UNVSViewWornSlots , UNVSViewLootBody , UNVSViewCrewGear , UNVSViewInventorySource In the Class Settings picker, each one appears under its Blueprint name, such as NVS View Meter Source for INVSViewMeterSource . The context id Every function takes an FGuid , named SetId , ContainerId , RecordId and so on. It tells a source that serves several sets which one is wanted. A source that serves one set, like BPC_Vitals , ignores it. For a binding as a screen opens, the id is made from the binding id with FGuid::NewDeterministicGuid , so Stash always gives the same id. When a screen asks about one record, such as the trader the player picked, the id is made from that record's id. UNVSViewDefinitionLibrary::ContextFor(FName RecordId) returns the same value in C++. When a screen reads again A screen reads every binding when it opens, again straight after it raises a request, and whenever Refresh Source names its source. RefreshViewModels() on a screen re-reads all of its bindings and is Blueprint callable. The market screen also asks its market source once a second. INVSViewContainerSource A grid container's description and its items. Each open container gets its own viewmodel, told apart by the context. bool DescribeContainer(const FGuid& ContainerId, FNVSViewContainerDescription& OutDescription) Fill the container's label, name and size. Return false for an unknown container and leave OutDescription untouched. void GetContainerItems(const FGuid& ContainerId, TArray<FNVSViewContainerItem>& OutItems) Fill the items. Reset OutItems first so an unknown container reads as empty. FNVSViewContainerDescription Meaning FText Label , FText ContainerName , FText NoteText The container's kind (such as BACKPACK), its name and a note line. FIntPoint GridSize Size in cells. bool bSearched False veils the contents until the player searches. Defaults to true. bool bOnBody The container is on a body, which shortens the search. bool bLocked Locked shut, like a secure case on a body. Contents are hidden and nothing goes in or out. FGameplayTag Place Where its items sit, under NVS.View.Place , which decides their menu rows. Empty counts as the stash. FGameplayTagQuery Accepts , FText RefusalText Which items it takes, and what the player is told when it refuses one. Empty takes anything. FNVSViewContainerItem Meaning FName ItemId Your handle for the item. Requests name it. FIntPoint TopLeft , FIntPoint Footprint Top-left cell, and size in cells with rotation already applied. FText DisplayName , FText TagText , FText SecondaryValue The name, the short tag and the figure under the name. Empty fields are not drawn. ENVSViewColorRole TintRole The tile's tint. bool bQuestLit , bool bUnaffordable An item an active task wants, and one the player cannot afford. FGameplayTagContainer ItemTags The item's own tags, which categories, slots and containers match against. FGameplayTag FitsSlot , FGameplayTag BlocksSlot The doll slot it is worn in, and the slot it rules out while worn. Both under NVS.View.Slot . TSoftObjectPtr<UTexture> RenderArt A picture for the inspect window. bool bUsable Offers a Use row. Your game applies the effect. FName InsideId The container this item opens, such as a pack. None when it opens nothing. FGameplayTag AttachKind The attachment kind it is, such as Attachment.Optic . Empty for an item that is not an attachment. int64 Price , int32 Count What "}, {"t": "Requests", "u": "requests.html", "s": "Every NVS.View.Request tag Blackline raises, its payload, which built-in sources answer it, and how your game answers the rest in Blueprint or C++.", "h": ["How a request travels", "Answer a request in Blueprint", "Answer a request in C++", "What the built-in sources answer", "The payload", "Every request", "History and replay", "What you see"], "x": "Screens never change game data themselves. A button that buys, crafts or heals raises a request, a gameplay tag under NVS.View.Request , on the NVS View Request Subsystem . The built-in sources answer most requests on their own, and your game binds On Request to answer the rest or to mirror the change in its own systems. How a request travels The player presses a button, drops an item or picks a menu row. The screen fills an FNVSViewRequest and calls Raise on its local player's request subsystem. The subsystem keeps the request in its history and broadcasts OnRequest . Every listener runs during the broadcast. Your game changes its own state here, or sends the change to its server. The screen offers the same request to every source it holds. A built-in source that knows the request changes its rows, such as the body zones healing on Heal all. The screen re-reads its sources and redraws, so a change made inside the broadcast shows at once. The subsystem is a local player subsystem, so each local player has its own. Nothing is sent over the network. Answer a request in Blueprint The demo's player controller answers its requests this way. It handles Raid.Deploy by streaming in the chosen raid map, Raid.TimeOut by bringing the player home, and Task.ShowOnMap by opening the location screen. Get the subsystem In your player controller's Begin Play, add Get NVSViewRequestSubsystem , the local player subsystem getter, with Self as the player controller. Bind the event Drag from it and choose Bind Event to On Request . Create a custom event from the red pin. It receives one NVS View Request . Check the tag Break the request and compare Request Id with the tag you answer, using Equal (Gameplay Tag) and a Branch, or a Switch on Gameplay Tag for several. Use the payload Read Subject Id , Item Id or whichever fields the request uses. For Raid.Deploy , Subject Id names the raid map, and Get Raid Map Definition gives you its level. You can add requests of your own. Make a gameplay tag, such as MyGame.Request.MarkJunk , and name it as the Action of an Item Action Definition. The item menu then offers the row and raises your tag when it is chosen. The demo adds a Mark as junk row exactly this way and answers Demo.Request.MarkJunk with a toast. Your own code can also call Raise on the subsystem, which is Blueprint callable. Answer a request in C++ // MyUIBridge.h #pragma once #include \"Subsystems/NVSViewRequestSubsystem.h\" #include \"MyUIBridge.generated.h\" UCLASS() class UMyUIBridge : public UObject { GENERATED_BODY() public: void Bind(ULocalPlayer* Player); private: UFUNCTION() void HandleRequest(const FNVSViewRequest& Request); }; // MyUIBridge.cpp #include \"MyUIBridge.h\" void UMyUIBridge::Bind(ULocalPlayer* Player) { if (UNVSViewRequestSubsystem* Requests = Player->GetSubsystem<UNVSViewRequestSubsystem>()) { Requests->OnRequest.AddDynamic(this, &UMyUIBridge::HandleRequest); } } void UMyUIBridge::HandleRequest(const FNVSViewRequest& Request) { if (Request.RequestId == NVSViewRequests::RaidDeploy()) { LoadRaid(Request.SubjectId); // Your own code. SubjectId names the raid map. } else if (Request.RequestId.MatchesTag(TAG_NVS_View_Request_Market)) { SendToMarketServer(Request); // Every Market request is a child of this tag. } } The NVSViewRequests namespace has a function for every built-in tag, such as NVSViewRequests::ItemMove() . The native tags themselves are declared in Tags/NVSViewTags.h , so you can match a whole family with MatchesTag . When your answer arrives later, for example from a server, call Refresh Source with the object that changed, or RefreshViewModels() on the open screen. See When a screen reads again . What the built-in sources answer A game that keeps its data in the plugin's built-in sources and the Inventory component needs no request code for these: Area Requests answered By Items Item.* , Bench.LoadPart , Station.Feed , the item side of Market.* and Trade.Swap The Inventory component, when the screen shows one. Trader deals change cash as the goods change hands. A loaded part and a fed fuel can leave the stash. Cash Cosmetic.Buy , the reward on Job.HandIn The session wallet, NVSViewCash . Each request is paid once however many sources hear it. Crafting Bench.Start , Bench.Collect , Bench.Upgrade , Station.Collect The hideout sources, from your Station Definitions. A craft starts only while the generator has fuel. An upgrade needs every part loaded and the cash in the wallet, then spends both. Health Health.HealAll , and Item.Use with a body zone as subject UNVSViewBodyZones . Heal all spends cash, and treating a zone uses the med. Tasks Job.Take , Job.HandIn , Task.Track The trader jobs and the task log, from your Task Definitions. Handing in takes the items from the player's inventory. Skills Perk.Take , Perk.Refund The perk tree and the player profile, which spends or refunds points. Wardrobe Cosmetic.Wear , Cosmetic.Buy , Cosmetic.ResetLook The wardrobe, the operator and the character looks. Loadouts Loadout.Save , Loadout.Apply The saved loadouts. Applying kits the player up from what the stash holds. Market Market.* The offline UNVSViewMarketBoard . Your game still hears every one of these on On Request , so it can save, log or mirror them. These are left to your game: Raid.Deploy , Raid.TimeOut , Raid.Report , Task.ShowOnMap and Notify.Act . The payload Every request is one FNVSViewRequest . Each request uses a few fields and leaves the rest at their defaults. Field Meaning FGameplayTag RequestId What the screen asks for, such as NVS.View.Request.Item.Move . FName SubjectId The record the request is about, such as a perk, a trader, a recipe or a raid map. None when there is no record. FName ItemId , FText ItemName , FText ItemTag The item the request moves or acts on. FIntPoint ItemFootprint , bool bTurned The item's size in cells, already turned when bTurned says it lands a quarter turn from how it lies now. FName TargetId Where the item goes. A doll slot for Item.Equip "}, {"t": "Project settings", "u": "project-settings.html", "s": "Where Blackline's project-wide choices live: the View Setup and Engine Settings Setup data assets, every field with its default, and the per-player values it saves.", "h": ["The two setup assets", "View Setup Definition", "Game", "Style and layers", "Render", "Money", "Load", "Words and slots", "Loot", "Engine Settings Setup Definition", "Per-player values", "Other config"], "x": "Blackline keeps its project-wide choices in two data assets instead of a Project Settings page. One sets the theme, the screens, icon rendering, money and load. The other names the sound classes and input assets the settings screen drives. This page lists every field. The two setup assets Data asset Class Module The plugin's own View Setup Definition UNVSViewSetupDefinition NVSView /NVSBlackline/Settings/Setup/DA_BlacklineSetup Engine Settings Setup Definition UNVSViewEngineSetupDefinition NVSViewSettings /NVSBlackline/Settings/Setup/DA_BlacklineEngineSetup The plugin's own assets use the defaults below. To make your own choices, create your own setup asset in your project. The plugin looks for it by itself, and a setup in your project always wins over the one the plugin ships. If you make several View Setup Definitions, the one with the highest Sort Order wins. Create it In your Content folder, right-click and choose Miscellaneous > Data Asset , then pick View Setup Definition . Name it, for example PDA_MySetup . Change what you need A new asset starts with the same defaults as the plugin's, which point at the plugin's own style, layout and screen registry. Change only the fields you care about. Press Play The screens use your setup from then on. There is nothing to register. Do the same with an Engine Settings Setup Definition when you want to change the audio or input assets. One engine setting does belong in Project Settings. CommonUI routes its input through its own viewport client, so set Project Settings > Engine > General Settings > Game Viewport Client Class to CommonGameViewportClient . The demo project is set up this way. View Setup Definition Game Field Default What it does GameTitle Empty The wordmark on the hub. Empty uses the project's name. KeyArt Empty The picture beside the hub. Empty shows no picture. RegionImage Empty The picture of the whole region on the location screen, under one point per raid map. Empty draws a plain grid. Style and layers Field Default What it does StyleAsset /NVSBlackline/DA_BlacklineStyle The theme every component uses unless its own Style property overrides it. If it cannot load, components fall back to built-in colours with no font, and LogNVSView warns. See Theming . LayoutAsset /NVSBlackline/DA_BlacklineLayout The layer table the layer stack is built from. ScreenRegistry /NVSBlackline/DA_BlacklineScreenRegistry The screens Show Screen By Tag can open, and the widget each screen tag opens. Point a row at your own Widget Blueprint to replace a screen everywhere. Render Field Default What it does IconsPerFrame 2 (1 to 16) The most item icons rendered in one frame. The rest wait in a queue. IconCacheMegabytes 64 (at least 8) Above this size, icons nobody is showing are dropped, oldest first. IconMaxPixels 512 (64 to 4096) The largest an item icon is drawn, along its long side. Raise it for sharp icons in big tiles at 4K. IconMargin 1.08 (0.5 to 3) How much room is left around an item in its icon. 1 fits it edge to edge. IconCutoutMaterial /NVSBlackline/Render/M_IconCutout The UI material that cuts a capture out of its background. It takes the capture as its Render parameter. KeyLightIntensity , FillLightIntensity , RimLightIntensity , SkyLightIntensity 6, 2, 4, 1 The brightness of the lights item icons and figure previews are captured under. KeyLightDirection , FillLightDirection , RimLightDirection (-40, -35, 0), (-15, 145, 0), (-25, 180, 0) Where the main light, the softer fill light and the edge light come from, as pitch, yaw and roll. See Rendering . Money Field Default What it does CurrencySymbol € Written before every amount. bSymbolAfterAmount false Puts the symbol after the amount with a space, as in 5,200 kr. ThousandsSeparator , Goes between each group of three digits. Load Field Default What it does LoadTiers SPRINT to 12 kg, JOG to 18 kg, WALK to 24 kg The load track's tiers, fastest first, each with its word and the weight it ends at. Get Load Tier returns the tier the player's carried weight falls in, from 0 for the lightest, or -1 when over the last one. Your movement code decides what each tier allows. An empty list draws no track. HeavyBootsKg 1.2 Boots at or above this weight count as loud on the noise signature. 0 never does. NoisyLoadKg 18 A load above this adds a step to the noise signature. 0 never does. The demo project sets its tiers to SPRINT 20, JOG 28 and WALK 36 kg. Words and slots Field Default What it does TagNames Empty The word shown for a tag, such as a slot ( NVS.View.Slot.Primary ), a kind of item or a calibre. A tag not listed shows the last part of its name in capitals. AllFilterIcon all The icon on the filter tab that shows every item. SlotBodyZones Head, ears, eyes and face to head . Armour, rig and pack to chest . Gloves and watch to arms . Legs, boots and sidearm to legs . The part of the body the character preview turns to when the player points at a slot's gear. A slot not listed gets no turn. Loot Field Default What it does SearchBaseSeconds 0.8 Seconds every search takes before the container's size counts. SearchSecondsPerCell 0.12 Seconds each cell of a container adds to its search. SearchBodyFactor 0.6 Searching a body's gear takes this share of the time a crate of the same size would. Engine Settings Setup Definition These name the assets the settings screen's engine-backed rows drive. The audio defaults point at assets in /NVSBlackline/Settings/Audio/ . Replace them with your own, or parent your game's sound classes under the shipped ones. See Settings . Field Default What it does SoundMix SCM_NVSSettings The sound mix the volume rows adjust. MasterClass , MusicClass , EffectsClass , VoiceClass , InterfaceClass SC_NVSMaster , SC_NVSMusic , SC_NVSEffects , SC_NVSVoice , SC_NVSInterface The sound class each volume row drives. NightModeEffect SFX_NVSNightMode Added to the master submix while dynamic range is set to Night. bDriveFieldOfView true Scales each player's camera field of view by their sav"}, {"t": "FAQ", "u": "faq.html", "s": "Answers to what buyers ask before and after buying Blackline: multiplayer, empty screens, Blueprint, the demo, gamepad, performance, limits and licences.", "h": ["Before you buy", "What is in the plugin?", "Does a fresh install show anything?", "What is the demo, and how do I get it?", "Which engine version and plugins does it need?", "Does it work in multiplayer?", "Does it support split screen?", "Can I use it without C++?", "Does it support gamepads?", "What does it cost at runtime?", "What are the limits I should know about?", "What licence does the bundled font have?", "What licence do the example characters have?", "After you buy", "How do I open a screen?", "How do I fill a screen that shows its empty state?", "Can I edit the shipped screens?", "How do I change the colours and font?", "Can I localise the screens?", "Something does not show. Where do I start?"], "x": "Short answers to the questions people ask about Blackline, taken from what the plugin's code does today. Where a limit exists, the answer says so. Before you buy What is in the plugin? The NVSBlackline plugin has five modules. NVSView , runtime. The layer stack, viewmodels, interfaces and built-in sources, the data asset types, the Inventory component, the raid session, deploy queue, request and notification subsystems, the style asset and the gameplay tags. NVSViewWidgets , runtime. The screens, the components and the screen library. NVSViewRender , runtime. The icon renderer and the character preview. NVSViewSettings , runtime. Engine-backed settings, saved settings, key rebinding and the field of view modifier. NVSViewEditor , editor only. Its content folder holds 27 screen and window Widget Blueprints, 48 component Widget Blueprints, the layer table DA_BlacklineLayout , the screen registry DA_BlacklineScreenRegistry , the theme DA_BlacklineStyle , the two setup assets, 17 context menu rows as Item Action Definitions, the JetBrains Mono font, 62 icon textures, the render materials, five character preview profiles, and the sound classes and input actions the settings screen uses. See Screens and Components . Does a fresh install show anything? The screens work, but your project has no content yet. Screens that list content, such as traders, the hideout or the stash, show an empty state card instead of sample data. It tells the player there is nothing here yet and, outside Shipping builds, tells you which data assets to create. The settings screen works from the start. See Empty states . What is the demo, and how do I get it? The free Blackline Demo is a small playable raid loop built on the plugin. You pick a map, deploy, loot a crate and a body, extract, then craft, trade, heal and kit up at home. It is built only from Blueprints and data assets. It comes in three downloads. BlacklineDemo-Win64.zip , a playable Windows build. BlacklineDemo-Linux.zip , a playable Linux build. BlacklineDemo-Project.zip , the Unreal project, with its Blueprints and data assets for you to open and copy from. It opens on /Game/Maps/L_BlacklineDemo . The project zip does not include the free FPS Weapon Bundle from Fab, because its licence does not allow passing it on. Add it from Fab so it lands at /Game/FPS_Weapon_Bundle , or the demo's guns have no model. See Getting started . Which engine version and plugins does it need? Unreal Engine 5.8. The plugin builds for Windows and Linux. Its descriptor enables three engine plugins, CommonUI, ModelViewViewModel and EnhancedInput. It needs no other marketplace plugin. Does it work in multiplayer? No, it is a single player kit as shipped. The Inventory component does not replicate, and the plugin declares no replicated properties and no RPCs. Its subsystems belong to a local player (layer stack, viewmodels, requests, notifications, deploy queue, raid session, settings) or to the game instance (icon renderer), and the icon renderer is not created on a dedicated server. A networked game can still use the screens if it does the networking itself: Supply your own replicated inventory through the NVS View Item Holder interface, which the Inventory component also implements. See Interfaces . Feed other screens from your replicated state with sources of your own. Requests are raised on the local player only. Send the change to your server from your On Request handler, then call Refresh Source when the answer arrives. The deploy queue is a local timer. It does not matchmake. Does it support split screen? Each local player gets their own layer stack, viewmodels, requests and notifications, because they are local player subsystems. Call Show Screen By Tag with each player's controller and each gets their own stack. Can I use it without C++? Yes. Showing screens, creating content, feeding live values and answering requests all work in Blueprint. The screen library's nodes ( Show Screen By Tag , Open Loot Screen , Reveal Hud Group , Set Extract Progress , Refresh Source ), the definition library's Set Screen Source and Set Selected Raid Map , the raid session's Begin Raid and End Raid , the request subsystem's On Request and Raise , and the Inventory component's functions are all Blueprint nodes. The demo's game is built from Blueprints and data assets only. Does it support gamepads? Partly. Buttons are CommonUI buttons, so they take focus and follow CommonUI navigation, and the gamepad's right face button closes a screen like Escape does. Many item interactions are written for a mouse, such as dragging between grids, right-clicking for the item menu and the hover shortcut keys. Test the screens you use with a pad before you ship. What does it cost at runtime? A screen reads its sources when it opens and when something changes. Widgets update through FieldNotify when a viewmodel field changes. The icon renderer captures item icons in an isolated studio world, at most IconsPerFrame per frame (2 by default), and caches them up to IconCacheMegabytes (64 by default). See Project settings . The market screen reads its source again once a second while it is open. Hidden raid HUD groups are collapsed, so they cost nothing to draw. What are the limits I should know about? Single player only, as above. The raid HUD's ammo readout shows the fitted magazine's capacity. It does not count shots. See Weapons . Cash, hideout progress, tasks and saved kits last for the play session. Your game saves them if they should survive a restart. Blackline pays only a task's cash. Its experience, reward lines and unlocks are text for your game to act on. The plugin does not load levels. Your game opens the raid map's level when the raid begins. What licence does the bundled font have? JetBrains Mono, under the SIL Open Font License 1.1. The licence text ships in the plugin at Licenses/JetBrainsMono-OFL.txt , is credited in CREDITS.md , and the plugin's filter keeps both in a packaged copy. What licenc"}, {"t": "Troubleshooting", "u": "troubleshooting.html", "s": "Fixes for screens showing their empty state, guns with no model in the demo, a missing theme, screens that do not open, stuck input and more, with the log lines that point at them.", "h": ["Read the log first", "A screen shows its empty state", "Guns have no model in the demo project", "Text is in the engine's default font, or the theme looks wrong", "The character is a mannequin of basic shapes, or empty", "A figure made of cylinders and a sphere", "Nothing at all", "A screen does not open", "From Show Screen By Tag or Show Screen", "From a button on another screen", "A panel stays blank after you set your own source", "Input stays in menu mode after the last screen closes", "Escape closes the wrong thing, or nothing", "The raid loop does not go where you expect", "A notification never goes away", "The hideout does not do what you expect", "Things that are not bugs", "Still stuck"], "x": "Most problems with Blackline leave a line in the Output Log. Start there, find the symptom below, and apply the fix. Read the log first The plugin logs on two categories. LogNVSView covers layers, screens, sources and settings. LogNVSViewRender covers icons and the character preview. Warnings show at the default verbosity. To see routine messages too, type these in the console and filter the Output Log on NVSView : Log LogNVSView Verbose Log LogNVSViewRender Verbose A screen shows its empty state A card such as No traders yet over a screen means the screen found nothing to show. That is what a project with no content looks like, and nothing is broken. Outside Shipping builds, the accent-coloured line under the title names what to create. Screen What to add Traders, Trade Trader Definitions. For the trade screen, give the trader a Shelf . Market Item Definitions with a price, or your own market source. Hideout, Workbench, Generator Station Definitions with levels and recipes, including one for the generator. Location select Raid Map Definitions. Skills, Skill tree Skill Definitions or Perk Definitions, with Perk Branch Definitions. Task log Task Definitions given to a trader. A task shows once the player takes it. Loadouts Loadout Definitions. Character Wardrobe Piece Definitions, including a body piece. Stash An Inventory component on the player's pawn, with starting items or a starting loadout. Debrief A call to End Raid on the raid session before the debrief opens. If you have added the assets and the card still shows: Check the asset is really of that class. Open it and look at the class name at the top of the Details panel. Check the asset's list is not empty. A Station Definition with no recipes still leaves the workbench empty. For the stash, check the Inventory component is on the pawn the player controls, and that the screen's Shows Player Inventory is still ticked. If you edited a screen's Empty When Bindings , look for ... decides its empty state by the binding '...', which it does not hold, so it never shows one. The id must match a binding in the same screen's View Model Bindings . To change the words or turn an empty state off, see Set or change an empty state . Guns have no model in the demo project The demo's guns, magazines, scope and grenade use models from the free FPS Weapon Bundle on Fab. The project zip leaves it out, because its licence does not allow passing it on. Without it, those items have no rendered picture and the first-person gun is missing. Get the bundle Add the FPS Weapon Bundle to your Fab library. Add it to the demo project Add it from Fab into BlacklineDemo so it lands at /Game/FPS_Weapon_Bundle . The item definitions look for it at exactly that path. Restart the editor Then press Play and open the stash. Until then, LogNVSViewRender warns Look asset ... did not load; it is left out. once for each missing mesh. The playable Windows build already has the bundle cooked in. Text is in the engine's default font, or the theme looks wrong Every component takes its colours, sizes and font from a style asset. When a component's own Style is unset, it uses StyleAsset on the View Setup Definition in force. If that does not load, components fall back to built-in colours with no font, so text draws in Slate's default font, and LogNVSView warns No style asset loaded from '...' . Find the setup in force A View Setup Definition in your project wins over the plugin's DA_BlacklineSetup . If you made one, check its StyleAsset names an asset that exists. The default is /NVSBlackline/DA_BlacklineStyle . Check the font Open the style asset and check its font is set. The shipped one uses /NVSBlackline/Fonts/F_JetBrainsMono . Check overrides A Style set on a screen or component wins over the setup. Clear it to use the setup's. The character is a mannequin of basic shapes, or empty A figure made of cylinders and a sphere Your project has no Wardrobe Piece Definitions with a body piece, so the figure falls back to a mannequin of basic shapes. The example characters are not part of the plugin. They ship with the Blackline Demo project. Create Wardrobe Piece Definitions for your own character, including a body piece. See Character looks . Or copy the demo's Wardrobe and Examples folders into your project, keeping their paths. Nothing at all A mesh or material in the look did not load. LogNVSViewRender warns Look asset ... did not load; it is left out. The preview is drawn by the icon renderer, which is not created on a dedicated server. See Rendering . A screen does not open From Show Screen By Tag or Show Screen Log line on LogNVSView Fix ShowScreenByTag: no screen is registered under '...'. The tag has no row in the screen registry in force. If you made your own registry, copy every row you still need from DA_BlacklineScreenRegistry . ShowScreenByTag: '...' declares no layer. The widget in that registry row has no layer. Set a Layer Override on the row, or Layer Tag in the widget's class defaults. ShowScreen: ... declares no layer. Set Layer Tag in the screen's class defaults. CreateLayerStack needs a local player. The player controller you passed has no local player, such as a server's copy of a remote player. Call it on the owning client. CreateLayerStack has no layout asset. Set Layout Asset on your View Setup Definition, or pass a layout to Create Layer Stack . From a button on another screen Log line on LogNVSView Fix cannot open '...': it names no class that will load. The destination was moved or deleted. Re-pick it in the screen's Menu Destinations or tab entry. cannot open '...': there is no layer subsystem for its player. The screen has no local player. Open screens through the screen library so they get one. cannot open '...': that class declares no layer. Set Layer Tag in the destination's class defaults. ... sends '...' to a screen and its tree has no button of that name. A Menu Destinations entry names a button the Widget Blueprint no longer has. Restore the but"}];
